window.KHAN_NOTES = [{"slug":"osi","title":"OSI model","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Select a layer, then hover or tap any protocol or device to learn what it does and why it belongs there.","body":"\u003csection class=\"osi-map\" aria-label=\"The seven OSI layers\">\u003cdiv class=\"osi-map-guide\">\u003cspan>APPS & SERVICES\u003c/span>\u003cspan>SELECT A LAYER TO EXPAND\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdetails class=\"osi-layer\" name=\"osi-layer\" data-osi-layer=\"7\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003csvg class=\"osi-layer-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003crect x=\"4\" y=\"5\" width=\"24\" height=\"22\" rx=\"3\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M4 11h24M12 16l-4 3 4 3m8-6 4 3-4 3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-label\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-sr-only\">Layer 7: \u003c/span>Application\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The network services your apps use.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-layer-content\">\u003cp class=\"osi-hover-hint\">Hover, focus, or tap an item for what it is and why it belongs here. Press Esc to dismiss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Web, network &amp; monitoring\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-dns\" data-osi-item=\"dns\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-dns\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-dns\" aria-expanded=\"false\">DNS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-dns\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">DNS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Resolves names and other DNS records.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines name-service questions and answers. Looking up an IP address does not make DNS a Layer 3 protocol.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">UDP and TCP 53\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-dnssec\" data-osi-item=\"dnssec\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-dnssec\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-dnssec\" aria-expanded=\"false\">DNSSEC\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-dnssec\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">DNSSEC\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Adds cryptographic validation of DNS data origin and integrity.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it extends DNS records and validation. It does not encrypt DNS queries or move name resolution to another layer.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-doh\" data-osi-item=\"doh\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-doh\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-doh\" aria-expanded=\"false\">DNS over HTTPS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-doh\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">DNS over HTTPS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Carries DNS queries and responses through HTTPS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: the DNS service is carried inside another application protocol, HTTP. Encryption does not change the purpose of either application service.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually HTTPS 443\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-dot\" data-osi-item=\"dot\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-dot\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-dot\" aria-expanded=\"false\">DNS over TLS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-dot\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">DNS over TLS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Protects a DNS exchange with TLS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7 describes the DNS service; TLS supplies protection underneath. The protected DNS message still answers a name-service question.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually TCP 853\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-dhcp\" data-osi-item=\"dhcp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-dhcp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-dhcp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">DHCP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-dhcp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">DHCP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Leases IP addresses and supplies settings such as gateway and DNS servers.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it is a client/server configuration service carried over UDP/IP. A MAC address can identify a client; DHCP does not translate MAC addresses into IP addresses.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">DHCPv4: UDP 67 server / 68 client\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-http\" data-osi-item=\"http\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-http\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-http\" aria-expanded=\"false\">HTTP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-http\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">HTTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Defines web requests and responses.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: methods, URLs, headers, and response codes give meaning to application messages.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually TCP 80\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-https\" data-osi-item=\"https\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-https\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-https\" aria-expanded=\"false\">HTTPS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-https\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">HTTPS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">HTTP communication protected by TLS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7 describes the web-service meaning; TLS supplies protection underneath. Using TCP or UDP port 443 does not move HTTP to Layer 4.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually TCP 443; HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP 443\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ntp\" data-osi-item=\"ntp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ntp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ntp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">NTP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ntp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">NTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Synchronizes clocks across networked systems.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it exchanges time-service messages. Accurate time supports authentication and investigation timelines.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually UDP 123\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-snmp\" data-osi-item=\"snmp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-snmp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-snmp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SNMP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-snmp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SNMP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Queries and reports device-management information.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines management queries, responses, and notifications. SNMPv3 adds security features when configured appropriately.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually UDP 161 queries / 162 notifications\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-syslog\" data-osi-item=\"syslog\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-syslog\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-syslog\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Syslog\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-syslog\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Syslog\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Sends log messages from devices or applications to a collector.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines the log message service and format; Ethernet and IP only carry those messages.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">UDP 514; syslog over TLS commonly TCP 6514\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-sip\" data-osi-item=\"sip\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-sip\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-sip\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SIP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-sip\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SIP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Session Initiation Protocol establishes and controls calls or other multimedia sessions.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines application signaling messages. The word “session” in its name does not make it an OSI Layer 5 protocol.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Typically UDP/TCP 5060; TLS over TCP 5061\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-sql\" data-osi-item=\"sql\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-sql\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-sql\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SQL Server / TDS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-sql\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SQL Server / TDS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Tabular Data Stream carries Microsoft SQL Server client requests and results.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines database-service messages. SQL itself is a query language, not a transport protocol.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Common default TCP 1433; instances can use other ports\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Windows, identity &amp; remote access\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-smb\" data-osi-item=\"smb\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-smb\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-smb\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SMB\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-smb\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SMB\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides file sharing and related network services.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines file operations and service messages. Its internal session setup does not move all of SMB to Layer 5.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually TCP 445\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-rdp\" data-osi-item=\"rdp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-rdp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-rdp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">RDP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-rdp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">RDP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides remote desktop interaction.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines the remote display and user-input service.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Typically TCP and UDP 3389\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ssh\" data-osi-item=\"ssh\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ssh\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ssh\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SSH\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ssh\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SSH\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides encrypted remote login and related services.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines remote-access messages and services. Being encrypted does not make the whole protocol Layer 6.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 22\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ldap\" data-osi-item=\"ldap\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ldap\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ldap\" aria-expanded=\"false\">LDAP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ldap\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">LDAP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Queries and updates directory information, such as users and groups.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines directory-service operations and responses.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 389; Windows also uses UDP 389 for CLDAP discovery\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ldaps\" data-osi-item=\"ldaps\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ldaps\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ldaps\" aria-expanded=\"false\">LDAPS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ldaps\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">LDAPS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">LDAP carried inside TLS from the start of the connection.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: its directory operations remain LDAP application messages; TLS supplies protection.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 636; LDAP can also use StartTLS on 389\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-kerberos\" data-osi-item=\"kerberos\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-kerberos\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-kerberos\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Kerberos\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-kerberos\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Kerberos\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Uses tickets to authenticate clients and services.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines an authentication service. It does not become Layer 5 merely because authentication supports sessions.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP/UDP 88\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-radius\" data-osi-item=\"radius\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-radius\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-radius\" aria-expanded=\"false\">RADIUS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-radius\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">RADIUS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Centralizes authentication, authorization, and accounting for network access.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it exchanges authentication/accounting messages. The endpoint-to-switch 802.1X/EAPOL exchange is a separate Layer 2 exchange.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Commonly UDP 1812 authentication / 1813 accounting\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-tacacs\" data-osi-item=\"tacacs\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-tacacs\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-tacacs\" aria-expanded=\"false\">TACACS+\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-tacacs\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">TACACS+\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides centralized authentication, authorization, and accounting, often for network-device administrators.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines access-control requests and responses between a device and its authentication service.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 49\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ike\" data-osi-item=\"ike\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ike\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ike\" aria-expanded=\"false\">IKE · IPsec setup\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ike\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">IKE · IPsec setup\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Internet Key Exchange authenticates peers and negotiates keys and security associations for IPsec.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Listed with application/control protocols because its messages run above UDP. It sets up the security used by Layer 3 IPsec AH/ESP; distinguish the setup exchange from the protected IP traffic.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually UDP 500; UDP 4500 with NAT traversal\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-winrm\" data-osi-item=\"winrm\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-winrm\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-winrm\" aria-expanded=\"false\">WinRM\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-winrm\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">WinRM\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides Windows remote-management services.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it exchanges management messages using application protocols over HTTP or HTTPS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 5985 HTTP / 5986 HTTPS\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-rpc\" data-osi-item=\"rpc\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-rpc\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-rpc\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Windows RPC\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-rpc\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Windows RPC\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Lets Windows software request operations from a remote service.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7 describes the actual RPC service messages above the transport. Its conversation management can also illustrate the Layer 5 responsibility.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 135 endpoint mapper plus negotiated service ports\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-saml\" data-osi-item=\"saml\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-saml\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-saml\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SAML\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-saml\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SAML\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Exchanges identity and authentication assertions between services.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: these are application-level identity messages, typically carried using web protocols.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-oauth\" data-osi-item=\"oauth\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-oauth\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-oauth\" aria-expanded=\"false\">OAuth / OpenID Connect\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-oauth\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">OAuth / OpenID Connect\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">OAuth delegates authorization; OpenID Connect adds an identity layer for authentication.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: applications exchange tokens and identity/authorization messages, usually over HTTPS.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Files, mail &amp; legacy services\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ftp\" data-osi-item=\"ftp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ftp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ftp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">FTP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ftp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">FTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Transfers files using separate control and data connections.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines file-transfer commands and replies. Traditional FTP does not protect credentials or content in transit.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 21 control; TCP 20 active-mode data; passive mode negotiates data ports\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-sftp\" data-osi-item=\"sftp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-sftp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-sftp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SFTP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-sftp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SFTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Transfers and manages files through SSH.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: file-service operations run inside SSH. SFTP is a different protocol from FTP with TLS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">Usually TCP 22\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ftps\" data-osi-item=\"ftps\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ftps\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ftps\" aria-expanded=\"false\">FTPS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ftps\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">FTPS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Adds TLS protection to FTP.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: the application is still FTP; TLS protects its connections. Separate data connections still matter.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 21 explicit TLS; TCP 990 implicit TLS control\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-tftp\" data-osi-item=\"tftp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-tftp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-tftp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">TFTP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-tftp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">TFTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A small file-transfer protocol often used for boot files or device images.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines file-transfer requests and acknowledgments over UDP. It does not provide authentication or encryption.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">UDP 69 initial request; transfer uses negotiated UDP ports\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-telnet\" data-osi-item=\"telnet\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-telnet\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-telnet\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Telnet · legacy\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-telnet\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Telnet · legacy\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides remote text-terminal access without built-in encryption.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines the terminal service. Recognize it in exams and legacy environments; SSH is the usual secure alternative.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 23\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-smtp\" data-osi-item=\"smtp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-smtp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-smtp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SMTP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-smtp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SMTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Transfers email between mail systems and accepts submitted email from clients.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines mail-transfer commands and responses. TLS can protect the connection separately.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 25 transfer; TCP 587 submission, commonly with STARTTLS\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-smtps\" data-osi-item=\"smtps\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-smtps\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-smtps\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SMTPS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-smtps\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SMTPS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">SMTP submission protected using TLS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it is a mail-submission application service. The N10-009 table labels 587 as SMTPS; operationally, 587 commonly upgrades with STARTTLS while 465 starts with implicit TLS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 587 submission / STARTTLS; TCP 465 implicit TLS\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-imap\" data-osi-item=\"imap\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-imap\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-imap\" aria-expanded=\"false\">IMAP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-imap\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">IMAP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Lets a client access and synchronize messages stored on a mail server.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines mailbox and message-access operations.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 143; TCP 993 with implicit TLS\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-pop3\" data-osi-item=\"pop3\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-pop3\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-pop3\" aria-expanded=\"false\">POP3\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-pop3\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">POP3\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Lets a client retrieve messages from a mail server.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it defines an email-retrieval service, rather than routing or local frame delivery.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 110; TCP 995 with implicit TLS\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Devices &amp; security functions\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-proxy\" data-osi-item=\"proxy\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-proxy\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-proxy\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Proxy server\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-proxy\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Proxy server\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Acts as an intermediary for application requests, such as web browsing.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">An application proxy works at Layer 7 because it understands and handles the application protocol.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-waf\" data-osi-item=\"waf\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-waf\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-waf\" aria-expanded=\"false\">WAF\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-waf\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">WAF\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A web application firewall inspects web requests and responses for application attacks.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: it uses HTTP content such as paths, parameters, and headers, beyond IP addresses and ports.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-l7-lb\" data-osi-item=\"l7-lb\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-l7-lb\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-l7-lb\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Layer 7 load balancer\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-l7-lb\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Layer 7 load balancer\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Distributes application requests, for example according to HTTP hostname or path.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 7: choosing by application content requires understanding the request, unlike a transport-only load balancer.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ngfw\" data-osi-item=\"ngfw\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ngfw\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ngfw\" aria-expanded=\"false\">NGFW\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ngfw\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">NGFW\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A next-generation firewall combines traffic filtering with application-aware security features.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Its application inspection reaches Layer 7, while address, port, and connection-state checks also use Layers 3–4. Name the function instead of assigning the whole device one layer.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ids\" data-osi-item=\"ids\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ids\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ids\" aria-expanded=\"false\">IDS / IPS\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ids\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">IDS / IPS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Detects suspicious traffic; an inline IPS can also block traffic according to policy.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Application-aware detection can inspect Layer 7, but IDS/IPS rules can also examine Layer 3/4 behavior. The placement here describes application inspection, not an exclusive device layer.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"osi-interview-line\">Interview answer: these protocols define what an application asks for and how the service responds. Their TCP/UDP port numbers still belong to Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"osi-study-links\">\u003csummary>Explore the protocol study notes\u003c/summary>\u003cnav aria-label=\"Layer 7 protocol studies\">\u003ca href=\"#note-ftp\">FTP\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-telnet\">Telnet\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-imap\">IMAP / IMAPS\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-pop3\">POP3 / POP3S\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-snmp\">SNMP\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-smtp\">SMTP / SMTPS\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-rpc-netbios\">RPC / NetBIOS\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-http\">HTTP / HTTPS\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-ssh\">SSH\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-rdp\">RDP\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-smb\">SMB\u003c/a>\u003ca href=\"#note-ldap\">LDAP / LDAPS\u003c/a>\u003c/nav>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\n\u003cdetails class=\"osi-layer\" name=\"osi-layer\" data-osi-layer=\"6\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">6\u003c/span>\u003csvg class=\"osi-layer-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003crect x=\"7\" y=\"14\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"3\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M11 14V9a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v5m-5 5v4\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-label\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-sr-only\">Layer 6: \u003c/span>Presentation\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Formats and protects the data.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-layer-content\">\u003cp class=\"osi-hover-hint\">Hover, focus, or tap an item for what it is and why it belongs here. Press Esc to dismiss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Protection &amp; representation\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-tls\" data-osi-item=\"tls\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-tls\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-tls\" aria-expanded=\"false\">TLS · protection role\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-tls\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">TLS · protection role\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Encrypts application traffic and authenticates peers as configured.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A common Layer 6 teaching example because it protects the representation of application data. Internet TLS does not map perfectly to one OSI layer; it commonly sits above TCP and is integrated with QUIC for HTTP/3.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ssl\" data-osi-item=\"ssl\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ssl\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ssl\" aria-expanded=\"false\">SSL · obsolete\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ssl\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">SSL · obsolete\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">The deprecated predecessor of TLS.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Historically grouped with Layer 6 for its encryption/protection role. Recognize the name in older diagrams; modern deployments should use supported TLS.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-utf8\" data-osi-item=\"utf8\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-utf8\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-utf8\" aria-expanded=\"false\">UTF-8 / ASCII\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-utf8\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">UTF-8 / ASCII\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Character encodings that specify how text is represented as bytes.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 6 describes representation. These are encodings, not TCP/UDP transport protocols.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-json\" data-osi-item=\"json\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-json\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-json\" aria-expanded=\"false\">JSON\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-json\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">JSON\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A text format for structured data exchanged by applications.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Its formatting role illustrates presentation responsibilities. JSON itself is a data format, not a network transport protocol.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-jpeg\" data-osi-item=\"jpeg\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-jpeg\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-jpeg\" aria-expanded=\"false\">JPEG\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-jpeg\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">JPEG\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A format and compression method for images.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Its job is to represent image data, which illustrates Layer 6 presentation. It does not route or deliver packets.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-compression\" data-osi-item=\"compression\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-compression\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-compression\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Compression\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-compression\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Compression\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Represents information using fewer bytes when the chosen method permits it.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Changing data representation is a presentation responsibility. Compression can be implemented at different points in a real stack.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-tls-termination\" data-osi-item=\"tls-termination\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-tls-termination\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-tls-termination\" aria-expanded=\"false\">TLS termination · function\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-tls-termination\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">TLS termination · function\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">An endpoint or proxy ends a TLS connection and processes the protected data.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">The TLS-processing part illustrates Layer 6 protection. The same proxy may inspect application requests at Layer 7.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"osi-interview-line\">Interview answer: Layer 6 handles representation and protection. Explain TLS as a common teaching example, while remembering that IPsec protects traffic at Layer 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\n\u003cdetails class=\"osi-layer\" name=\"osi-layer\" data-osi-layer=\"5\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5\u003c/span>\u003csvg class=\"osi-layer-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cpath d=\"M4 5h19v14H12l-5 5v-5H4zM23 11h5v14h-8l-4 4v-4h-3v-6\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-label\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-sr-only\">Layer 5: \u003c/span>Session\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Keeps track of an ongoing conversation.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-layer-content\">\u003cp class=\"osi-hover-hint\">Hover, focus, or tap an item for what it is and why it belongs here. Press Esc to dismiss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Session concepts &amp; examples\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-session-setup\" data-osi-item=\"session-setup\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-session-setup\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-session-setup\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Session setup / teardown\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-session-setup\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Session setup / teardown\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Starts and ends the context of an ongoing conversation.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 5 describes managing that dialogue. Modern applications usually implement this responsibility themselves.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-session-state\" data-osi-item=\"session-state\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-session-state\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-session-state\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Session state\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-session-state\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Session state\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Keeps track of the context needed across related exchanges, such as an ongoing login.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">This illustrates Layer 5’s session responsibility. A login session can last across several TCP connections; it is not the same thing as one transport connection.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-checkpoint\" data-osi-item=\"checkpoint\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-checkpoint\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-checkpoint\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Checkpoints / recovery\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-checkpoint\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Checkpoints / recovery\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Records progress so a conversation or transfer can resume from an agreed point when supported.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Synchronization and recovery of an ongoing dialogue are session-layer responsibilities.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-netbios\" data-osi-item=\"netbios\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-netbios\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-netbios\" aria-expanded=\"false\">NetBIOS session service\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-netbios\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">NetBIOS session service\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A legacy service that establishes named, connection-oriented sessions between systems.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A common Layer 5 teaching example because it establishes and manages sessions. NetBIOS also includes other services, so do not classify every NetBIOS function the same way.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">NetBIOS over TCP session service: TCP 139\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-rpc-session\" data-osi-item=\"rpc-session\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-rpc-session\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-rpc-session\" aria-expanded=\"false\">RPC · conversation role\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-rpc-session\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">RPC · conversation role\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Remote Procedure Call lets software ask another system to perform an operation.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Managing the request/reply dialogue illustrates session responsibilities. Actual RPC protocols run in the application stack over transports; this is a functional teaching example, not an exclusive Layer 5 classification.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"osi-interview-line\">Interview answer: Layer 5 manages conversations and context. There is no required “session-layer box,” and SIP is still an application protocol despite its name.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"osi-study-links\">\u003csummary>Explore the protocol study notes\u003c/summary>\u003cnav aria-label=\"Layer 5 protocol studies\">\u003ca href=\"#note-rpc-netbios\">RPC / NetBIOS\u003c/a>\u003c/nav>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\n\u003cdetails class=\"osi-layer\" name=\"osi-layer\" data-osi-layer=\"4\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4\u003c/span>\u003csvg class=\"osi-layer-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003crect x=\"3\" y=\"8\" width=\"7\" height=\"17\" rx=\"2\"/>\u003crect x=\"22\" y=\"8\" width=\"7\" height=\"17\" rx=\"2\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M10 12h12m-4-3 4 3-4 3M22 21H10m4-3-4 3 4 3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-label\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-sr-only\">Layer 4: \u003c/span>Transport\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Delivers data to the right service.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-layer-content\">\u003cp class=\"osi-hover-hint\">Hover, focus, or tap an item for what it is and why it belongs here. Press Esc to dismiss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Protocols\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-tcp\" data-osi-item=\"tcp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-tcp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-tcp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">TCP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-tcp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">TCP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides a connection-oriented, ordered, reliable byte stream.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 4: it carries data between service endpoints using ports, sequencing, acknowledgments, and retransmissions.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-udp\" data-osi-item=\"udp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-udp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-udp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">UDP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-udp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">UDP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Sends individual datagrams without providing TCP’s delivery and ordering guarantees.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 4: it uses ports to deliver datagrams to the appropriate endpoint. Applications can add reliability above it.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-quic\" data-osi-item=\"quic\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-quic\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-quic\" aria-expanded=\"false\">QUIC · transport role\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-quic\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">QUIC · transport role\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides secure, reliable streams over UDP; HTTP/3 uses it.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">It performs transport responsibilities while running above UDP and integrating TLS. It does not fit a strict one-protocol-per-OSI-layer chart.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Hardware &amp; functions\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-stateful-firewall\" data-osi-item=\"stateful-firewall\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-stateful-firewall\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-stateful-firewall\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Stateful firewall\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-stateful-firewall\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Stateful firewall\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Tracks connection/flow state to decide which traffic to allow.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 4 describes its TCP/UDP and port-based inspection. It also uses Layer 3 addresses; more advanced inspection can reach Layer 7.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-l4-lb\" data-osi-item=\"l4-lb\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-l4-lb\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-l4-lb\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Layer 4 load balancer\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-l4-lb\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Layer 4 load balancer\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Distributes connections among servers using addresses, ports, and transport information.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 4: it can choose a server without interpreting an HTTP URL or other application message.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ports\" data-osi-item=\"ports\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ports\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ports\" aria-expanded=\"false\">TCP / UDP ports\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ports\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">TCP / UDP ports\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Software numbers that help identify service endpoints on a host.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">They are fields in Layer 4 TCP/UDP headers. TCP 443 is a service endpoint number; switch jack 4 is a physical interface.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"osi-interview-line\">Interview answer: TCP and UDP are transport protocols. An application using port 53 or 443 does not become a Layer 4 application.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\n\u003cdetails class=\"osi-layer\" name=\"osi-layer\" data-osi-layer=\"3\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3\u003c/span>\u003csvg class=\"osi-layer-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cellipse cx=\"16\" cy=\"16\" rx=\"13\" ry=\"10\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M5 16h7m-3-3 3 3-3 3m18-3h-7m3-3-3 3 3 3M16 6v7m-3-3 3 3 3-3m-3 16v-7m-3 3 3-3 3 3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-label\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-sr-only\">Layer 3: \u003c/span>Network\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Moves packets between networks.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-layer-content\">\u003cp class=\"osi-hover-hint\">Hover, focus, or tap an item for what it is and why it belongs here. Press Esc to dismiss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Protocols &amp; routing\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ipv4\" data-osi-item=\"ipv4\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ipv4\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ipv4\" aria-expanded=\"false\">IPv4\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ipv4\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">IPv4\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Addresses packets and supports delivery between IP networks.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: source and destination IP addresses support routing beyond the local link.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ipv6\" data-osi-item=\"ipv6\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ipv6\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ipv6\" aria-expanded=\"false\">IPv6\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ipv6\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">IPv6\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides 128-bit IP addressing and routed packet delivery.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: it identifies IP endpoints and networks so packets can be forwarded between links.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-icmp\" data-osi-item=\"icmp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-icmp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-icmp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">ICMP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-icmp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">ICMP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Carries IPv4 diagnostic and error messages; ping commonly uses echo messages.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: it reports IP-delivery conditions and is carried directly by IP. Ping is not a TCP-port test.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">IP protocol 1; no TCP/UDP ports\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-icmpv6\" data-osi-item=\"icmpv6\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-icmpv6\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-icmpv6\" aria-expanded=\"false\">ICMPv6 / NDP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-icmpv6\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">ICMPv6 / NDP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Carries IPv6 control messages. Neighbor Discovery finds neighbors and routers and resolves link-layer addresses.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Grouped with Layer 3 because NDP is carried by ICMPv6 inside IPv6, even though neighbor resolution helps local delivery. IPv6 uses NDP rather than ARP.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">IPv6 Next Header 58; not a port\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-igmp\" data-osi-item=\"igmp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-igmp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-igmp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">IGMP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-igmp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">IGMP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Manages IPv4 multicast-group membership between hosts and nearby routers.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: it controls which IP multicast groups a host wants to receive.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ipsec\" data-osi-item=\"ipsec\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ipsec\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ipsec\" aria-expanded=\"false\">IPsec\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ipsec\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">IPsec\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A suite that protects IP traffic, commonly used in VPNs.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: its AH and ESP mechanisms protect IP traffic. Encryption does not automatically mean Layer 6.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ah\" data-osi-item=\"ah\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ah\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ah\" aria-expanded=\"false\">AH\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ah\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">AH\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">IPsec Authentication Header provides integrity and authentication, without encrypting the payload.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: AH is an IPsec header carried by IP, not an application service on a TCP/UDP port.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">IP protocol 51; not port 51\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-esp\" data-osi-item=\"esp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-esp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-esp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">ESP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-esp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">ESP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">IPsec Encapsulating Security Payload can encrypt and authenticate protected traffic.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: ESP protects IP traffic. NAT traversal can wrap ESP in UDP without changing its IPsec role.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">IP protocol 50; NAT-T can use UDP 4500\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-gre\" data-osi-item=\"gre\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-gre\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-gre\" aria-expanded=\"false\">GRE\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-gre\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">GRE\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Generic Routing Encapsulation carries another protocol inside an IP tunnel.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: the tunnel is delivered using an outer IP header. GRE by itself does not encrypt traffic.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">IP protocol 47; not port 47\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ospf\" data-osi-item=\"ospf\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ospf\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ospf\" aria-expanded=\"false\">OSPF\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ospf\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">OSPF\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Open Shortest Path First exchanges routing information inside a routing domain.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3 control: it builds routes for IP forwarding and runs directly over IP.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">IP protocol 89; no TCP/UDP port\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-eigrp\" data-osi-item=\"eigrp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-eigrp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-eigrp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">EIGRP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-eigrp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">EIGRP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Enhanced Interior Gateway Routing Protocol exchanges routes between routers.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3 control: it supports IP route selection and is carried directly by IP.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">IP protocol 88; not port 88\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-bgp\" data-osi-item=\"bgp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-bgp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-bgp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">BGP · routing role\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-bgp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">BGP · routing role\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Border Gateway Protocol exchanges routes between routing domains.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Its purpose is Layer 3 routing, so it is listed here by function. Its messages run over TCP, making its protocol-stack placement application layer. In an interview, distinguish routing purpose from how the protocol is carried.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">TCP 179\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Hardware &amp; functions\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-router\" data-osi-item=\"router\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-router\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-router\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Router\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-router\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Router\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Forwards packets between IP networks.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 3: it examines the destination IP address and chooses a next hop using its routing table.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-l3-switch\" data-osi-item=\"l3-switch\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-l3-switch\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-l3-switch\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Layer 3 switch\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-l3-switch\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Layer 3 switch\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Combines Ethernet switching with IP routing, often between VLANs.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Its routed forwarding is Layer 3. Ordinary switching within a VLAN remains a Layer 2 function.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-firewall-ip\" data-osi-item=\"firewall-ip\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-firewall-ip\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-firewall-ip\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Firewall · IP filtering\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-firewall-ip\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Firewall · IP filtering\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Permits or denies traffic according to security rules.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Address-based filtering uses Layer 3. Port filtering uses Layer 4, and application inspection can use Layer 7. A firewall is not limited to one layer.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-nat\" data-osi-item=\"nat\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-nat\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-nat\" aria-expanded=\"false\">NAT / PAT\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-nat\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">NAT / PAT\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Translates IP addresses; PAT also translates transport-port mappings.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">NAT changes Layer 3 addresses. PAT additionally uses Layer 4 ports, so distinguish the address-only and address-plus-port functions.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"osi-interview-line\">Interview answer: a router forwards packets using destination IP addresses. Layer 3 uses IP protocol numbers, not TCP/UDP service ports.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\n\u003cdetails class=\"osi-layer\" name=\"osi-layer\" data-osi-layer=\"2\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2\u003c/span>\u003csvg class=\"osi-layer-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003crect x=\"2\" y=\"8\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" rx=\"3\"/>\u003cpath d=\"M6 13h4v6H6zm8 0h4v6h-4zm8 0h4v6h-4zM8 24v5m16-5v5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-label\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-sr-only\">Layer 2: \u003c/span>Data link\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Moves frames across a local link.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-layer-content\">\u003cp class=\"osi-hover-hint\">Hover, focus, or tap an item for what it is and why it belongs here. Press Esc to dismiss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Protocols &amp; standards\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-arp\" data-osi-item=\"arp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-arp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-arp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">ARP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-arp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">ARP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Address Resolution Protocol finds a MAC address for a known IPv4 next-hop address on the local link.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Usually grouped with Layer 2: ARP is carried directly in Ethernet, without TCP or UDP. It connects Layer 3 addressing to Layer 2 delivery, so some references call it an L2/L3 boundary protocol. It does not assign IP addresses.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-ports\">No TCP/UDP ports\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ethernet\" data-osi-item=\"ethernet\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ethernet\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ethernet\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Ethernet\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ethernet\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Ethernet\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Defines local frames, including source and destination MAC addresses.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">The framing and MAC-addressing functions are Layer 2. Ethernet standards also specify Layer 1 signaling.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-wifi\" data-osi-item=\"wifi\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-wifi\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-wifi\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wi-Fi / 802.11\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-wifi\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Wi-Fi / 802.11\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Defines wireless link access and wireless frames.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">The MAC/framing function is Layer 2; the radio and signaling function is Layer 1. State which part you mean.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-dot1q\" data-osi-item=\"dot1q\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-dot1q\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-dot1q\" aria-expanded=\"false\">802.1Q / VLANs\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-dot1q\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">802.1Q / VLANs\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Separates switched traffic into VLANs; tags identify VLANs on links that carry tagged frames.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 2: VLAN membership controls the local frame-forwarding and broadcast domain, rather than IP routing.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-stp\" data-osi-item=\"stp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-stp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-stp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">STP / RSTP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-stp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">STP / RSTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Spanning Tree protocols prevent forwarding loops by controlling redundant switch paths.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 2: they organize the topology used to forward Ethernet frames.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-lacp\" data-osi-item=\"lacp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-lacp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-lacp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">LACP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-lacp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">LACP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Link Aggregation Control Protocol coordinates multiple physical links in a logical bundle.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 2: its control messages manage a link aggregation group between adjacent systems.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-lldp\" data-osi-item=\"lldp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-lldp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-lldp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">LLDP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-lldp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">LLDP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Link Layer Discovery Protocol advertises a device’s identity and capabilities to directly connected neighbors.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 2: these neighbor-discovery messages stay on the local link instead of being IP-routed.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-cdp\" data-osi-item=\"cdp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-cdp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-cdp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">CDP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-cdp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">CDP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Cisco Discovery Protocol advertises information about directly connected Cisco-capable neighbors.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 2: its discovery frames describe the local connection; routers do not forward them as ordinary IP packets.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-dot1x\" data-osi-item=\"dot1x\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-dot1x\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-dot1x\" aria-expanded=\"false\">802.1X / EAPOL\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-dot1x\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">802.1X / EAPOL\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Controls access to a network port; EAP over LAN carries authentication exchanges on the local link.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 2 describes the EAPOL exchange between an endpoint and switch or access point. The device may separately contact a RADIUS server using an application-layer protocol.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-wpa\" data-osi-item=\"wpa\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-wpa\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-wpa\" aria-expanded=\"false\">WPA2 / WPA3\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-wpa\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">WPA2 / WPA3\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Security suites that protect Wi-Fi access and wireless traffic.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Their Wi-Fi link protection belongs with Layer 2. They do not replace application encryption such as HTTPS.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Hardware\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-switch\" data-osi-item=\"switch\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-switch\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-switch\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Switch\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-switch\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Switch\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Connects devices and forwards Ethernet frames using a MAC-address table.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A basic switch is Layer 2 because its forwarding decision uses the destination MAC address within a VLAN.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-bridge\" data-osi-item=\"bridge\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-bridge\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-bridge\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Bridge\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-bridge\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Bridge\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Connects link segments and filters or forwards frames between them.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 2: it uses MAC addresses. A conventional Ethernet switch is effectively a multiport bridge.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-ap\" data-osi-item=\"ap\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-ap\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-ap\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Wireless access point\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-ap\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Wireless access point\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Connects Wi-Fi clients to a network, commonly bridging wireless and wired traffic.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Its bridging function is Layer 2. Its radio is Layer 1; routing in an all-in-one Wi-Fi router is a separate Layer 3 function.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-nic\" data-osi-item=\"nic\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-nic\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-nic\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Network adapter / NIC\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-nic\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Network adapter / NIC\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Provides a device’s network interface and handles link-layer frames.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Its MAC and framing functions are Layer 2. The same adapter also performs Layer 1 transmission and reception.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"osi-interview-line\">Interview answer: a switch forwards frames by MAC address. ARP resolves IPv4 to a local MAC; DHCP assigns network configuration.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\n\u003cdetails class=\"osi-layer\" name=\"osi-layer\" data-osi-layer=\"1\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1\u003c/span>\u003csvg class=\"osi-layer-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cpath d=\"M3 11h8v12H3zm18 0h8v12h-8zM11 17h10M6 7v4m2-4v4m16-4v4m2-4v4M6 23v4m20-4v4\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-label\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-sr-only\">Layer 1: \u003c/span>Physical\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Carries signals through cables or air.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-layer-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-layer-content\">\u003cp class=\"osi-hover-hint\">Hover, focus, or tap an item for what it is and why it belongs here. Press Esc to dismiss.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-concept-card osi-item-group\">\u003ch3>Hardware\u003c/h3>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-item-list\">\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-hub\" data-osi-item=\"hub\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-hub\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-hub\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Hub\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-hub\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Hub\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Connects devices by repeating incoming electrical signals to the other ports.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1: it repeats signals without examining MAC addresses or IP addresses. This is the classic distinction from a switch.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-repeater\" data-osi-item=\"repeater\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-repeater\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-repeater\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Repeater\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-repeater\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Repeater\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Regenerates a signal so it can travel farther.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1: it works with the physical signal, rather than choosing where a frame or packet should go.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-copper\" data-osi-item=\"copper\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-copper\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-copper\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Copper cable\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-copper\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Copper cable\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Carries electrical signals between network interfaces.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1: the cable is the physical medium that carries bits.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-fiber\" data-osi-item=\"fiber\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-fiber\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-fiber\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Fiber cable\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-fiber\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Fiber cable\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Carries information as pulses of light.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1: it transports the signal; it does not interpret MAC or IP addresses.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-sfp\" data-osi-item=\"sfp\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-sfp\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-sfp\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Transceiver / SFP\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-sfp\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Transceiver / SFP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A module that sends and receives signals, often converting electrical signals to optical signals.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1: its transmission function turns bits into signals for the chosen medium. SFP describes a module form factor.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-patch-panel\" data-osi-item=\"patch-panel\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-patch-panel\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-patch-panel\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Patch panel\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-patch-panel\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Patch panel\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">A passive panel that organizes and terminates cabling.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1: it connects physical cable paths. It does not learn MAC addresses or route traffic.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-modem\" data-osi-item=\"modem\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-modem\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-modem\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Modem\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-modem\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Modem\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Modulates and demodulates signals for a communications link.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1 describes that signal-conversion function. A home modem/router combination also performs higher-layer jobs.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-item\">\u003cbutton class=\"osi-item-button\" type=\"button\" data-study-tip=\"osi-tip-radio\" data-osi-item=\"radio\" aria-describedby=\"osi-tip-radio\" aria-controls=\"osi-tip-radio\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Radio / antenna\u003cspan aria-hidden=\"true\">?\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip\" id=\"osi-tip-radio\" role=\"tooltip\" popover=\"manual\">\u003cstrong class=\"osi-tooltip-title\">Radio / antenna\u003c/strong>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHAT IT IS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Transmits and receives wireless signals.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-label\">WHY HERE · LAYER 1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"osi-tooltip-copy\">Layer 1: this is the physical radio part of Wi-Fi. Wi-Fi framing and access-point bridging also involve Layer 2.\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"osi-interview-line\">Interview answer: a hub repeats signals. It does not make forwarding decisions from addresses.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdiv class=\"osi-map-guide osi-map-base\">\u003cspan>CABLES & SIGNALS\u003c/span>\u003cspan>7 LAYERS · ONE NETWORK\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/section>\u003cp class=\"osi-map-note\">Study the function, not just the label: hub → signals; switch → MAC addresses; router → IP addresses. Some entries span layers or illustrate a responsibility; their explanations call that out.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"osi-map-note\">Coverage draws on Network+ N10-009 and Security+ SY0-701 objectives, plus the role’s Windows, networking, identity, and security-monitoring requirements.\u003c/p>","question":"Why is a hub Layer 1, a switch Layer 2, and a router Layer 3? Where does ARP fit?","answer":"A hub repeats signals. A basic switch forwards frames using MAC addresses. A router forwards packets using IP addresses. ARP is normally grouped with Layer 2 because it is carried directly on the link; it resolves an IPv4 next-hop address to a MAC address, connecting the Layer 3 and Layer 2 responsibilities.","sources":["netObjectives","secObjectives","osiStandard","internetLayers","lanDevices","windowsPorts","arp","tlsProtocol","http3","ipsecOSI","bgpOSI","ospfOSI","eigrpOSI","sipOSI","mailTLS","ikeOSI","dnssecOSI","dohOSI","dotOSI"],"related":["switching","routing","tcp","dns","tls","arp"],"keywords":"OSI seven 7 layers physical data link network transport session presentation application TCP/IP MAC IP ARP hub repeater bridge switch router access point NIC firewall proxy WAF load balancer DHCP DNS DNSSEC DoH DoT BGP OSPF EIGRP IPsec IKE GRE AH ESP STP LACP LLDP CDP EAPOL 802.1X RADIUS TACACS Kerberos LDAP LDAPS SSH FTP SFTP FTPS TFTP SMTP IMAP POP3 SNMP syslog RDP SIP Network+ Security+ interview protocol studies FTP Telnet IMAP POP3 SNMP SMTP RPC NetBIOS HTTP SSH RDP SMB LDAP","references":[{"title":"CompTIA Network+ N10-009: official exam objectives","url":"https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-network-n10-009-exam-objectives-(4-0)-(1).pdf"},{"title":"CompTIA Security+ SY0-701: official exam objectives","url":"https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-security-sy0-701-exam-objectives-(6-0).pdf"},{"title":"ITU-T X.200: the OSI basic reference model","url":"https://www.itu.int/rec/T-REC-X.200-199407-I/en"},{"title":"RFC 1122: Internet host requirements and the TCP/IP layers","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1122.html"},{"title":"Cisco: hubs, bridges, and switches","url":"https://www.cisco.com/en/US/docs/internetworking/troubleshooting/guide/tr1923.html"},{"title":"Microsoft: Windows service overview and network port requirements","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/service-overview-and-network-port-requirements"},{"title":"RFC 826 — Address Resolution Protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc826"},{"title":"RFC 8446: Transport Layer Security 1.3","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8446/"},{"title":"RFC 9114 — HTTP/3","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114"},{"title":"RFC 4301: IPsec architecture","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4301"},{"title":"RFC 4271: BGP routing and its TCP transport","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4271"},{"title":"RFC 2328: OSPF","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2328"},{"title":"RFC 7868: EIGRP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7868"},{"title":"RFC 3261: SIP as an application-layer signaling protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3261"},{"title":"RFC 8314: TLS for email submission and access","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8314"},{"title":"RFC 7296: IKEv2 and its UDP-carried key exchange","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7296"},{"title":"RFC 4033: DNS security extensions","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4033"},{"title":"RFC 8484: DNS over HTTPS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8484"},{"title":"RFC 7858: DNS over TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7858"}]},{"slug":"private-addressing","title":"Private IPv4 & NAT","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A private IP identifies a device inside a network. NAT lets devices using private addresses communicate through an internet-facing address.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with the address\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An IPv4 address is four numbers separated by dots, such as \u003ccode>10.0.0.25\u003c/code>. Each number can be 0–255. Your laptop needs an address so other devices can send traffic to it. A \u003cstrong>private\u003c/strong> address can be reused inside different homes and businesses because it is not routed directly across the public internet.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Private range\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10.0.0.0–10.255.255.255\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.0.0.25\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>172.16.0.0–172.31.255.255\u003c/td>\u003ctd>172.20.5.10\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>192.168.0.0–192.168.255.255\u003c/td>\u003ctd>192.168.1.20\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A 10.x address is just as private as a 192.168.x address. The administrator or router configuration chooses which range to use. Only 172.16 through 172.31 is private in the 172 range. Also notice the spelling: \u003ccode>196.168\u003c/code> is different from \u003ccode>192.168\u003c/code>. Addresses outside these ranges are not automatically public; other special ranges exist.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What NAT actually changes\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Network Address Translation (NAT)\u003c/strong> changes addresses as traffic passes through a router or firewall. In a typical office, the device changes a laptop’s private source address into the address used on the internet side. \u003cstrong>Port Address Translation (PAT)\u003c/strong> also tracks port numbers, allowing many laptops to share one public address. A port identifies a particular service or connection on a device.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Laptop                  Office gateway              Website\n10.0.0.25:53010    →     203.0.113.10:62001     →     TCP 443\n                        Translation recorded\nReply to :62001   →     matches the record     →     laptop :53010\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>The gateway keeps a translation table so replies return to the right laptop. The public address above is reserved for documentation. Real connections can also pass through another translation at the internet provider.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Different networks can share an internet connection\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An office might put staff in \u003ccode>10.10.10.0/24\u003c/code> and guests in \u003ccode>192.168.20.0/24\u003c/code>. Both can use the same public internet address. Creating another VLAN or subnet does not automatically create or require another public IP. Internal separation and internet address translation are separate settings.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on a support or security ticket\u003c/h2>\u003cp>If a user reports “my IP is 10.x instead of 192.168.x,” that alone is not a fault. Compare their address, subnet mask, gateway, and DNS servers with the intended network using \u003ccode>ipconfig /all\u003c/code>. When investigating an external service’s logs, its recorded source may be the office’s public address. Use the timestamp and translation or connection records to identify an internal device; do not assume that public address identifies one employee.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Remember\u003c/strong>\u003cp>NAT changes addresses. Firewall rules decide which traffic is allowed. A malicious program can still make outbound connections through NAT.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Do a staff VLAN and a guest VLAN need separate public IP addresses?","answer":"No. They can use different private subnets and share a public address through PAT. Their separation depends on VLAN configuration and the rules controlling traffic between them, not on having different public IPs.","sources":["ip","nat"],"related":["subnetting","vlans","firewalls","vpns"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 1918 — Private address space","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918"},{"title":"RFC 3022 — Traditional NAT","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3022"}]},{"slug":"subnetting","title":"Subnetting","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Practiced calculations","principle":"Subnetting divides an IP address range into smaller networks. The subnet mask tells a device which addresses are local and which need a router.","body":"\u003ch2>What the slash means\u003c/h2>\u003cp>In \u003ccode>192.168.204.30/28\u003c/code>, the address identifies a device and \u003ccode>/28\u003c/code> describes its network size. IPv4 has 32 bits: 28 identify the network and the remaining 4 distinguish addresses inside it. A bit is one binary digit, 0 or 1. An \u003cstrong>octet\u003c/strong> is a group of eight bits: eight bits can represent 256 values, numbered 0–255.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>subnet mask\u003c/strong> writes those network bits as ones and the host bits as zeros. The /28 mask is \u003ccode>11111111.11111111.11111111.11110000\u003c/code>, or \u003ccode>255.255.255.240\u003c/code>. For a destination on the same subnet, a host usually communicates directly. For another subnet, it normally sends the packet to its default gateway, a router interface.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Count the addresses\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"formula\">Host bits = 32 − prefix\u003cbr>Total addresses = 2\u003csup>host bits\u003c/sup>\u003cbr>Ordinary usable addresses = total − 2\u003c/div>\u003cp>For /28: 4 host bits give 16 total addresses. The first is the \u003cstrong>network address\u003c/strong>, the label for the whole subnet. The last is the \u003cstrong>broadcast address\u003c/strong>, used to address the subnet’s devices collectively. That leaves 14 ordinary host addresses. A gateway uses one of those 14; it is not a bonus address.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Work through 192.168.204.30/28\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Find the mask: \u003ccode>255.255.255.240\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the increment in the changing octet: \u003ccode>256 − 240 = 16\u003c/code>. Fourth-octet blocks start at 0, 16, 32, 48, and so on.\u003c/li>\u003cli>30 sits in the 16–31 block. Network: \u003ccode>192.168.204.16\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The next block starts at .32, so broadcast is \u003ccode>192.168.204.31\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Usable addresses: \u003ccode>192.168.204.17–192.168.204.30\u003c/code>. The original address .30 is the last usable host.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"subnet\" aria-label=\"Move the network boundary\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Move the network boundary\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the prefix or enter an address. Predict the network and broadcast before revealing the calculation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>When the boundary is in the third octet\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>10.50.132.75/21\u003c/code>, 11 host bits give 2,048 total addresses. The mask is \u003ccode>255.255.248.0\u003c/code>. Its third-octet increment is \u003ccode>256 − 248 = 8\u003c/code>. The block containing 132 runs from 128 through 135.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Result\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Address\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Network\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.50.128.0\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Broadcast\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.50.135.255\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Usable range\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.50.128.1–10.50.135.254\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ordinary capacity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2,046 host addresses\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The increment is 8 in the third octet, while the entire subnet contains 2,048 addresses. These are different quantities. Calculate the broadcast from the network boundary, not by adding the increment to the original host address.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it at work\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For a department with 50 devices, /26 provides 62 ordinary host addresses, leaving room for a gateway and some growth. Subnetting also helps diagnose a wrong mask, an incorrect gateway, or an overly broad firewall rule. An address plan still needs gateways, DHCP settings, and switch configuration to become a working network.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Two exceptions\u003c/strong>\u003cp>The “minus two” rule describes ordinary IPv4 broadcast subnets. /31 point-to-point links can use both addresses; /32 identifies one address. Cloud platforms can reserve extra addresses.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Find the network, broadcast, mask, and ordinary usable capacity of 172.19.155.200/18.","answer":"The mask is 255.255.192.0. The increment is 64 in the third octet, so 155 sits in 128–191. Network: 172.19.128.0. Broadcast: 172.19.191.255. Usable range: 172.19.128.1–172.19.191.254, giving 16,382 addresses.","sources":["subnet","p31"],"related":["vlsm","dhcp","routing"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — IPv4 addressing and subnetting","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/routing-information-protocol-rip/13788-3.html"},{"title":"RFC 3021 — IPv4 /31 links","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3021"}]},{"slug":"vlsm","title":"VLSM","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Practiced calculations","principle":"Variable Length Subnet Masking gives each subnet a size that fits its needs, instead of giving every department the same-sized block.","body":"\u003ch2>Why use different sizes?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Suppose a warehouse needs 100 device addresses but a kiosk network needs only 12. Giving both a /25 would reserve 128 addresses for each. VLSM lets the warehouse use /25 and the kiosks use /28. Both subnets can fit inside a larger allocation without overlapping. Read \u003ca href=\"#note-subnetting\">Subnetting\u003c/a> first if network and broadcast addresses are still unfamiliar.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Choose a prefix from a requirement\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Choose enough host bits that \u003ccode>2^h − 2\u003c/code> covers the required host addresses. Then calculate \u003ccode>32 − h\u003c/code> for the prefix. For 100 devices, /26 gives only 62 usable addresses. /25 gives 126. Count the gateway, printers, management interfaces, and growth before finalizing a real plan.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Devices in this exercise\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Candidate\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Ordinary capacity\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>100\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/25\u003c/td>\u003ctd>126\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>50\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/26\u003c/td>\u003ctd>62\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>28\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/27\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>12\u003c/td>\u003ctd>/28\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Allocate the biggest blocks first\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>10.60.24.0/24\u003c/code> as the parent allocation. Start with the largest requirement and place the next subnet after the previous subnet’s broadcast address. Each start must also be a valid boundary for its prefix.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Department\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Subnet\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Usable range\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Broadcast\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Warehouse\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.0/25\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.1–.126\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.127\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Engineering\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.128/26\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.129–.190\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.191\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sales\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.192/27\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.193–.222\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.223\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Kiosks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.60.24.224/28\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.225–.238\u003c/td>\u003ctd>.239\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All shortened addresses keep the first three octets \u003ccode>10.60.24\u003c/code>. The final block, \u003ccode>10.60.24.240/28\u003c/code>, remains available. The first child can start at the same address as its parent: /24 describes the whole allocation, while /25 describes only its first half.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Catch the common mistakes\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>A /26 starts at a multiple of 64 in the fourth octet: .0, .64, .128, or .192. “The next free address” is not enough if it is misaligned.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The network and broadcast are not ordinary device addresses. In the kiosk subnet, .224 is the network, .239 is the broadcast, and .225–.238 are usable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The prefix is part of the network definition. Write \u003ccode>10.60.24.128/26\u003c/code>, not just “.128.”\u003c/li>\u003cli>Largest-first allocation is a practical method that avoids fragmentation. It is not a protocol requirement.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch2>Turn the plan into a working office\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Create the intended VLANs, assign their switch ports, and configure a router or Layer 3 switch interface for each subnet. Set up DHCP scopes with the matching mask, gateway, and DNS servers. Reserve or exclude statically assigned infrastructure addresses. Then apply rules for permitted traffic between departments and test both an allowed and a blocked connection. The calculation decides address capacity; these configuration steps implement it.\u003c/p>","question":"Why can’t 50 engineering devices use a /27, and what else should you count before choosing a /26?","answer":"A /27 has only 30 ordinary usable addresses. A /26 has 62. Count the gateway, other infrastructure, and expected growth as well as the 50 devices. Ensure the chosen block starts on a valid boundary and does not overlap another subnet.","sources":["subnet","p31"],"related":["subnetting","vlans"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — IPv4 addressing and subnetting","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/routing-information-protocol-rip/13788-3.html"},{"title":"RFC 3021 — IPv4 /31 links","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3021"}]},{"slug":"vlans","title":"VLANs","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A VLAN makes one physical switch behave like several separate local networks. Devices share a local broadcast network only when their ports belong to the same VLAN.","body":"\u003ch2>Picture an office switch\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A switch connects laptops, printers, and other devices by cable. Without separation, those devices may all share one local network. A \u003cstrong>Virtual Local Area Network\u003c/strong> assigns ports to separate groups. Staff can be in VLAN 10, guests in VLAN 20, and servers in VLAN 30, even when they connect to the same switch.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>broadcast\u003c/strong> is a message sent to every device in a local broadcast domain. For example, an ARP request asks which device owns a particular local IP. The switch floods that broadcast within its VLAN, not into every VLAN. This separation is the basic job of a VLAN.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"vlan\" aria-label=\"One switch. Separate networks.\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>One switch. Separate networks.\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Move the guest laptop between VLANs, then test its path to the server. Watch which boundary needs a routing policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Ports, tags, and subnets\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Term\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Access port\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Normally carries one VLAN for an end device. The attached laptop usually sends untagged frames.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trunk port\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Carries multiple VLANs between network devices, commonly using 802.1Q tags to identify each VLAN.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Subnet\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The IP address range used on a network. A common office design assigns a different subnet to each VLAN.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Inter-VLAN routing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A router or Layer 3 switch moves traffic between VLANs. Firewall rules or access control lists can restrict that traffic.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A VLAN and a subnet are different settings. The VLAN is the local Ethernet separation; the subnet tells IP devices what is local. They are normally planned together. Giving a laptop an address from a different subnet does not move its switch port into another VLAN.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What actually protects the server?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>If guests and servers are in different VLANs, a guest cannot directly reach the server through ordinary same-VLAN switching. But if a router connects the two networks and allows the traffic, the guest can still reach the server. The intended policy might allow guests to use the internet while denying connections to server addresses.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If an administrator accidentally places a guest port in the server VLAN, traffic may stay inside that VLAN and bypass the inter-VLAN firewall. Check VLAN membership as well as the rules between networks. Hosts and applications also need their own controls.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on a client ticket\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Confirm the device’s IP, mask, gateway, and physical switch port.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check the port’s VLAN and whether the expected VLAN is allowed on upstream trunks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check that this VLAN has the right gateway and DHCP scope or relay.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For cross-VLAN access, inspect the route and the rule for the specific source, destination, protocol, and port.\u003c/li>\u003cli>After an approved change, verify the intended connection works and a forbidden connection remains blocked.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Public IPs are separate\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Staff and guest VLANs can share the same public IP for internet access. A VLAN creates local separation, not a new internet subscription.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"A guest VLAN exists, but guests can still open the file server. What would you investigate?","answer":"Check that the guest is actually on the guest VLAN, then inspect routing and firewall or ACL rules between guests and servers. Separate VLANs alone do not guarantee a deny policy. Verify the exact service and both allowed and blocked paths.","sources":["vlan","subnet"],"related":["subnetting","ztna-sase","switching","firewalls","osi"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — Inter-VLAN routing and access rules","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/smb/routers/cisco-rv-series-small-business-routers/1393-Inter-VLAN-Routing-with-Targeted-ACL-Restrictions.html"},{"title":"Cisco — IPv4 addressing and subnetting","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/routing-information-protocol-rip/13788-3.html"}]},{"slug":"arp","title":"ARP","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"ARP finds the MAC address needed to deliver IPv4 traffic to the next device on a local network. For a remote destination, that device is usually your gateway.","keywords":"address resolution protocol MAC Ethernet broadcast cache next hop gateway spoofing poisoning","body":"\n\u003ch2>Why an IP address is not enough\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Your laptop wants to send data to a printer. It knows the printer’s IPv4 address, but an Ethernet network delivers local traffic using \u003cstrong>MAC addresses\u003c/strong>. A MAC address identifies a network interface on that link, often written as six hexadecimal pairs, such as \u003ccode>02:00:00:00:00:50\u003c/code>. It is different from the IP address and is not a permanent proof of device identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>IP packet\u003c/strong> carries the network-layer destination. For its trip across an Ethernet link, that packet sits inside a \u003cstrong>frame\u003c/strong> with local source and destination MAC addresses. \u003cstrong>Address Resolution Protocol (ARP)\u003c/strong> supplies the missing IPv4-to-MAC mapping for the next hop: the device that should receive this frame.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Ask the local network, then remember the reply\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Imagine a laptop at \u003ccode>10.20.10.25/24\u003c/code> and a printer at \u003ccode>10.20.10.50/24\u003c/code>, both on the same VLAN.\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\u003cli>The laptop checks its routing information. In this simple network, the printer is directly reachable on the local subnet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It checks its \u003cstrong>ARP cache\u003c/strong>, a temporary table of IP-to-MAC mappings. A usable entry can avoid a new request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Without an entry, it broadcasts an ARP request: “Who has 10.20.10.50? Tell 10.20.10.25.” The Ethernet destination is \u003ccode>ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff\u003c/code>, the broadcast address. The switch distributes it within that VLAN.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The printer normally replies directly to the laptop with its MAC address. The laptop caches the mapping and can address the frame to the printer.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Other devices can hear the request, but they do not normally answer for an address they do not own. ARP itself runs directly over the local link; it does not use a TCP or UDP port.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>What changes when the destination is remote?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Now the laptop wants to reach a website outside its subnet. With a default route through \u003ccode>10.20.10.1\u003c/code>, it resolves \u003cstrong>the gateway’s MAC address\u003c/strong>. It does not broadcast across the internet asking for the website’s MAC.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The frame is addressed to the gateway, while the packet inside still names the website’s IP as its destination. The router removes the incoming link-layer framing and forwards the packet using the next link’s addressing. Ordinary ARP broadcasts do not pass through routers. This is why understanding the subnet and gateway comes before interpreting an ARP lookup.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"arp\" aria-label=\"Choose the next hop\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Who should answer?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Choose a printer or a remote website. Follow the lookup, request, reply, and first frame. Send again to see what the cache changes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to step through the exercise. For the local printer, resolve 10.20.10.50; for the remote website, resolve gateway 10.20.10.1. A cached mapping skips the initial broadcast.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Keep the different tables straight\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Mechanism\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Question it answers\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DHCP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which IP configuration should this device use?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DNS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What address is associated with this name?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Routing table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which next hop and interface should carry this IP packet?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ARP cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which MAC address corresponds to that local IPv4 next hop?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Switch MAC table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which switch port leads toward this MAC address in this VLAN?\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2>Use it on a connectivity ticket\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A laptop has a valid address but cannot reach the gateway. Check its address, mask, gateway, and VLAN first. On Windows, \u003ccode>arp -a\u003c/code> displays cached mappings, separated by interface. Compare the relevant next-hop entry with the expected device, and inspect a capture with Wireshark’s \u003ccode>arp\u003c/code> display filter if needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Repeated requests without replies can suggest an offline next hop, wrong VLAN, incorrect addressing, or a filtering/link problem. Check the capture location too. A MAC entry proves neither that the application works nor that its IP owner is authentic. Record evidence before clearing a cache; “delete everything and retry” can hide a useful clue.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Address changes and security\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dynamic entries age or are refreshed according to the operating system. Devices can also announce an address mapping without first receiving an ordinary request, often called gratuitous ARP. ARP probes and announcements support address-conflict detection and legitimate changes such as failover, so a mapping change is not automatically an attack.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>ARP does not authenticate ownership of an address. False mappings, called \u003cstrong>ARP spoofing\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>cache poisoning\u003c/strong>, can redirect or disrupt local traffic. Investigate unexpected gateway mappings alongside device and switch evidence. On supported switches, Dynamic ARP Inspection validates ARP information against trusted bindings, commonly learned through DHCP snooping; static devices and trusted ports need appropriate configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Scope to remember\u003c/strong>\u003cp>This note describes ordinary IPv4 over Ethernet. Proxy ARP is a special case where a router answers on another device’s behalf. IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery rather than ARP.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Your laptop sends to a website outside its /24 subnet. Does ARP look up the website’s MAC address or the default gateway’s, and which IP remains inside the packet?","answer":"With the default route selected, ARP resolves the gateway’s local IPv4 address to its MAC. The Ethernet frame goes to that gateway, while the enclosed IP packet still names the website as its destination. A usable cached gateway mapping can avoid a new ARP request.","sources":["arp","arpConflict","arpWindows","arpInspection"],"related":["subnetting","vlans","dhcp","dns","switching","routing","osi"],"references":[{"title":"RFC 826 — Address Resolution Protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc826"},{"title":"RFC 5227 — IPv4 address conflict detection","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5227"},{"title":"Microsoft — View the ARP cache","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/administration/windows-commands/arp"},{"title":"Cisco — Dynamic ARP Inspection","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/switches/lan-switch-software/222274-troubleshoot-dynamic-arp-inspection-dai.html"}]},{"slug":"dns","title":"DNS","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"DNS translates names such as portal.example.com into information computers can use, often an IP address. It helps find a destination; it does not establish the connection.","body":"\u003ch2>Why names need a lookup\u003c/h2>\u003cp>People remember a name more easily than a server’s IP address. When an application needs an address, your computer checks available cached information and normally asks its configured \u003cstrong>DNS resolver\u003c/strong>. The resolver may answer from its cache or ask other DNS servers until it finds the authoritative answer. \u003cstrong>Authoritative\u003c/strong> means the server holds the published records for that DNS zone.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Caching saves repeated lookups. A record’s \u003cstrong>TTL\u003c/strong>, or time to live, tells a cache how long it may keep that record before checking again. This helps explain why a DNS change can appear to work on one device while another still uses an older answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Records you will encounter\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Record\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it tells you\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A / AAAA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The IPv4 / IPv6 address associated with a name.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>CNAME\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An alias pointing to another name.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>MX\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Which mail servers receive email for a domain.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>TXT\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Text used for purposes such as domain verification and email authentication policies.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>PTR\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A reverse lookup from an IP address to a name, when configured.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>SRV\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Where a particular service can be found; Active Directory uses these to locate domain services.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Separate a name problem from a service problem\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A user says websites fail, but your remote-support session still works. That proves some connectivity remains. It does not prove DNS is the cause: an existing session might not need a new lookup, and websites may use a different path or policy.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>ipconfig /all\nnslookup portal.example.com\nResolve-DnsName portal.example.com\nTest-NetConnection portal.example.com -Port 443\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>These Windows checks show the configured DNS servers, query a name, and test the service’s TCP port. Replace the example with the actual affected hostname. \u003ccode>Test-NetConnection\u003c/code> proves a TCP connection can be established, not that HTTPS or the application is healthy. Browsing to a bare IP can fail because certificates and website routing expect a hostname.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read the symptom before changing settings\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Useful next check\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Name does not exist / NXDOMAIN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check spelling and whether the expected record exists in the correct DNS zone.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Query times out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check reachability to the configured resolver and whether it answers other queries.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>An unexpected address is returned\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compare intended records, caches, split DNS, and recent changes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Correct address, but application fails\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check the service port, TLS, proxy, firewall, and application health.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Internal names may only exist on company DNS servers, often reachable through an approved remote-access path. Replacing corporate DNS with a public resolver can break domain logons or file-server lookups. Record the query, resolver, answer, and time before clearing a cache; that evidence can explain what happened.\u003c/p>","question":"A name resolves to the expected IP, but the website still fails. What has DNS proved?","answer":"It has provided the expected address for that lookup. It has not proved that the service port is reachable, the TLS handshake works, or the application is healthy. Test those separately and compare the scope of affected users.","sources":["dns"],"related":["dhcp","tls","vpns","osi"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Troubleshooting DNS clients","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/troubleshoot/troubleshoot-dns-client"}]},{"slug":"dhcp","title":"DHCP","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"DHCP automatically gives a device an IP address and the network settings it needs. The address is borrowed for a lease period, rather than manually typed into every computer.","body":"\u003ch2>What a new laptop needs\u003c/h2>\u003cp>When a laptop joins an office network, it needs an available IP address, a subnet mask, a default gateway for reaching other networks, and DNS servers for looking up names. \u003cstrong>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol\u003c/strong> supplies these settings. A home router often runs the DHCP service, but a business may use Windows Server, a firewall, or another network service. DHCP is not limited to routers.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>The first lease: DORA\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Discover:\u003c/strong> the client looks for a DHCP server. It does not yet have a usable lease for this network.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Offer:\u003c/strong> a server offers an address and configuration.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Request:\u003c/strong> the client requests the selected offer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Acknowledge:\u003c/strong> the server confirms the lease. The client can apply the settings after its normal checks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"dhcp\" aria-label=\"Watch a laptop join the network\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Watch a laptop join the network\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Step through the initial IPv4 lease exchange, then try it with the server unavailable.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003cp>This is the common initial IPv4 exchange. Renewing an existing lease usually starts with a Request, not a complete Discover/Offer cycle. DHCPv4 uses UDP ports 67 for the server and 68 for the client.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read a DHCP scope\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Setting\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example and purpose\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Scope\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The address pool and settings for one subnet: 10.20.30.0/24.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Offer 10.20.30.50–10.20.30.200 to clients.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exclusion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keep statically configured devices out of the offered pool.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reservation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Give a particular client a consistent address through DHCP, commonly identified by its MAC address or client identifier.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lease\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How long the client may use the assignment before it expires unless renewed.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Options\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Supply the gateway and DNS servers. Option 003 is routers; 006 is DNS servers in common Windows DHCP administration.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A reservation still depends on DHCP. A manually configured static address is set on the device itself. Plan both so two devices are not assigned the same address.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Why VLANs sometimes need a relay\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The first broadcast does not normally cross a router. If the DHCP server is on another subnet, a \u003cstrong>DHCP relay\u003c/strong> forwards the request and identifies the client’s network so the server can choose the right scope. A new VLAN may have perfectly good switch ports but no functioning DHCP path if its relay or scope is missing.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Troubleshoot “I have no network”\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Start with \u003ccode>ipconfig /all\u003c/code>. Check whether DHCP is enabled, which server supplied the lease, the address, mask, gateway, DNS servers, and lease times. On a Windows client configured for DHCP, an unexpected \u003ccode>169.254.x.x\u003c/code> link-local address is a clue that it could not obtain a lease. It is not proof of the exact failure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compare another device on the same VLAN. Check scope exhaustion, server health, relay settings, switch VLAN membership, and unexpected DHCP servers. A wrong DNS option can produce a valid IP but broken name lookups. Releasing an address can disconnect a remote-support session, so plan any renewal work around the user’s access.\u003c/p>","question":"Devices on one new VLAN get 169.254 addresses, while other VLANs work. What would you check?","answer":"Confirm the clients are using DHCP and are on the intended VLAN. Check that VLAN’s scope, available addresses, gateway relay, and path to the DHCP server. A failure limited to one VLAN points toward its configuration or path, though the address alone does not prove the cause.","sources":["dhcp","dhcpScope","dhcpRFC"],"related":["dns","vlans","switching"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — DHCP Server overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-top"},{"title":"Microsoft — DHCP scopes","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/dhcp/dhcp-scopes"},{"title":"RFC 2131 — DHCP messages and leases","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2131"}]},{"slug":"syslog","title":"Syslog","category":"Cloud & telemetry","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Syslog is a common way for devices and applications to send event messages to a central place. Instead of opening every firewall or server, an analyst can review their messages together.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with one event\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A switch port goes down. A firewall blocks a connection. A Linux service fails to start. Each system can write a message describing what happened. With syslog forwarding configured, it also sends that message to a \u003cstrong>collector\u003c/strong>: a server that receives logs from other systems.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>log\u003c/strong> is a recorded event. A collector stores or forwards those events. A \u003ca href=\"#note-siem\">SIEM\u003c/a> can search and analyze collected events and create alerts using rules. Receiving a syslog message does not automatically mean an attack was detected.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read the message in pieces\u003c/h2>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;134&gt;1 2026-09-05T10:15:00Z fw-01 firewall 812 DENY - Blocked TCP from 10.20.10.25 to 10.20.30.10:445\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>This fictional RFC 5424-style message records one blocked connection. \u003ccode>fw-01\u003c/code> identifies the reporting device; the timestamp says when it recorded the event. The final text explains the action, source, and destination. \u003ccode>445\u003c/code> is a service port commonly used for SMB file sharing.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Field\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">How to read it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>&lt;134&gt;\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Priority combines a facility and severity. Here 16 × 8 + 6 = 134: local0 facility, informational severity.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The syslog message format version.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2026-09-05T10:15:00Z\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Event time; Z means UTC.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>fw-01 / firewall / 812\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hostname, application name, and process identifier.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DENY / -\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Message identifier, then no structured data.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Blocked TCP…\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The human-readable event description. Vendor message bodies vary.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"syslog\" aria-label=\"Which messages reach the collector?\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Which messages reach the collector?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the forwarding threshold. Lower severity numbers are more urgent; the word “blocked” does not automatically mean “critical.”\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Severity is a label, not a verdict\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Syslog has eight severity levels: 0 emergency, 1 alert, 2 critical, 3 error, 4 warning, 5 notice, 6 informational, and 7 debug. The sender assigns a level. A normal firewall deny can be informational. An informational event can still matter to an investigation. If a device forwards only warning and more urgent messages, levels 5–7 may never reach the collector.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>How the messages travel\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The device needs a destination collector address, a transport and port, and a choice of messages to forward. Traditional UDP syslog commonly uses port 514. UDP provides no delivery acknowledgement, so messages can be lost. Syslog over TLS commonly uses TCP 6514 to protect the connection. Plain TCP alone does not encrypt the messages; the sender and receiver must support and agree on the chosen transport.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it in an investigation\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Suppose a user cannot reach a file server. Search the firewall’s collected events for the user’s source IP, the server’s destination IP, port 445, and the time of the attempt. A matching deny can identify the rule that blocked it. Record the rule and intended access before changing it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If no message appears, check whether the device generated it locally, whether its forwarding filter includes that severity, and whether the collector is receiving other events. Then check parsing, timestamps, and the search time range. “No results” can mean missing collection or a wrong query, not “nothing happened.”\u003c/p>","question":"A firewall shows a local informational deny event, but the collector only receives warning and more urgent events. Why is your search empty?","answer":"Informational is severity 6; warning is 4. A threshold that includes only 0–4 excludes that deny. Check the forwarding filter and collection path before concluding that the event did not happen. The sender’s severity is not an analyst’s final threat assessment.","sources":["syslog","syslogUDP","syslogTLS"],"related":["siem","firewalls","alert-triage","snmp"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 5424 — Syslog","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5424"},{"title":"RFC 5426 — Syslog over UDP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5426"},{"title":"RFC 5425 — Syslog over TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5425"}]},{"slug":"mdr","title":"EDR, MDR, and XDR","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"EDR watches devices. XDR brings signals from several security areas together. MDR provides people and processes to investigate and respond using those tools.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with the problem\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A user opens an attachment and something suspicious runs on their laptop. You need to know what ran, whether it caused harm, whether other accounts or devices are involved, and who will respond. EDR, XDR, and MDR address different parts of this work. They are not three mutually exclusive products or a required sequence of upgrades.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Understand each role\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Term\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it contributes\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Software records device activity such as processes, files, and network connections. It can detect threats and perform supported response actions.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SentinelOne or Microsoft Defender for Endpoint.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Extended Detection and Response (XDR)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A platform links related signals across areas such as endpoints, email, identities, and cloud applications.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Connecting a phishing email, a suspicious process, and a risky account sign-in into an investigation.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Managed Detection and Response (MDR)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A service supplies analysts and an agreed process for monitoring, investigation, and response. It uses its own and/or integrated tools.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A Blackpoint SOC analyst investigates an integrated SentinelOne alert and takes action within the contracted scope.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>endpoint\u003c/strong> is a device such as a laptop or server. \u003cstrong>Telemetry\u003c/strong> means the recorded activity it sends. A \u003cstrong>SOC\u003c/strong>, or security operations center, is the team and function that monitors and responds to threats. MDR adds that operational service; XDR describes a technology approach, not automatically a staffed team.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"response\" aria-label=\"Follow one suspicious attachment\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Follow one suspicious attachment\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Step through the same fictional incident to see what the endpoint tool, broader platform, and responding people contribute.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>How an EDR can feed an MDR service\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An endpoint agent sends activity and alerts to its product’s platform. A supported integration can share those alerts and relevant context with an MDR provider, often through an API: a structured connection between products. The provider’s analysts investigate with the available evidence and their own sensors or connected services. Depending on permissions, they may use that integration to stop a process or isolate a device.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a concrete example, Blackpoint documents a SentinelOne integration that monitors alerts, supports response, and writes resolution information back into SentinelOne. This requires a configured integration and service coverage. It is not a guarantee that every raw endpoint event or firewall log is included in every MDR plan.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Where XDR and a SIEM fit\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An XDR platform can connect the endpoint alert to an email delivered to the same person and a suspicious sign-in. A \u003ca href=\"#note-siem\">SIEM\u003c/a> provides broader log collection, search, and detection across configured sources. Their capabilities can overlap and integrate. Microsoft Sentinel is a separate product from SentinelOne; Sentinel and Defender XDR can share an investigation experience.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What an analyst still needs to do\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>Confirm the customer, affected device, user, timestamp, and evidence behind the alert.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read what automatic response or the MDR provider already completed. “Detected” is different from “successfully contained.”\u003c/li>\u003cli>Determine scope: other devices, mailbox activity, account use, and business impact.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Follow the response plan. Urgent containment can happen while investigation continues when the evidence and authority justify it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Document actions, owners, outstanding tasks, and the evidence needed before restoring access.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Do not equate the labels\u003c/strong>\u003cp>MDR does not simply mean “a place to view firewall logs.” The defining feature is the managed investigation and response service. Verify the actual data sources and responsibilities in the deployed service.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Why would an organization use SentinelOne and Blackpoint together instead of choosing only one?","answer":"SentinelOne supplies endpoint visibility and response controls. With the supported integration and coverage configured, Blackpoint can provide continuous monitoring, investigation, and response around those alerts and its other evidence. The organization still coordinates business impact, remediation, and follow-up. XDR can add broader correlation across security areas.","sources":["edr","xdr","blackpointS1","mdrEDR"],"related":["alert-triage","malware","incident-response"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"SentinelOne — Endpoint protection","url":"https://www.sentinelone.com/platform/endpoint-protection-platform/"},{"title":"Microsoft — Defender XDR overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/microsoft-365-defender"},{"title":"Blackpoint — SentinelOne integration","url":"https://blackpointcyber.com/integrations/sentinelone/"},{"title":"Blackpoint — Managed EDR","url":"https://blackpointcyber.com/why-blackpoint/managed-edr/"}]},{"slug":"siem","title":"SIEM","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A SIEM collects searchable security data from different systems and applies detection rules. It helps an analyst investigate a sequence of events across the environment.","body":"\u003ch2>Why collect logs together?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A firewall can show a blocked connection. A Windows server can show a failed logon. An email system can show message delivery. Opening each device separately is slow, especially across many customers. A \u003cstrong>Security Information and Event Management\u003c/strong> platform brings configured sources into a place where you can search and compare them. Microsoft Sentinel is one example.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Follow the data\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Generate:\u003c/strong> a source system records an event.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Collect:\u003c/strong> an agent, API connector, or log forwarder sends it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Parse:\u003c/strong> fields such as timestamp, account, IP, and action become searchable. Some connectors also normalize different vendor formats into common fields.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Detect:\u003c/strong> a rule evaluates the data, for example many failed logons followed by a success.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Investigate:\u003c/strong> an analyst examines the alert and related evidence. Several related alerts may be grouped into an incident.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>event\u003c/strong> records something that happened. An \u003cstrong>alert\u003c/strong> flags activity that matched detection logic. An \u003cstrong>incident\u003c/strong> is a unit of investigation that can group related alerts and entities. These are not interchangeable.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Work through a simple case\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Imagine 30 failed logons against one server, followed by a successful logon from the same source. A detection rule might raise an alert. The analyst asks whether the account normally signs in that way, what kind of logon succeeded, and what happened afterward. The pattern could indicate password guessing, but it could also involve a broken service followed by a legitimate user. The raw events and context support the conclusion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A rule must define its time window, grouping, exclusions, and threshold. Thirty failures against one user are a different question from thirty failures spread across many users. Tuning is the process of adjusting detection logic to make it useful while preserving visibility.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What a dashboard cannot prove\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>No alert does not prove no attack occurred. Data may be missing, a rule may not cover the behavior, or a threshold may not be reached.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A map based on an IP address estimates the address’s location. It does not identify the attacker’s physical location.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A colorful dashboard is a view of data. Verify source health, timestamps, and fields before trusting its interpretation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Central collection does not automatically grant response authority. Automated actions need configured permissions and an approved workflow.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch2>Use it during daily review\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Check the incident queue and source health. For an investigation, select the correct customer or workspace, define a time range, and search the account, device, or IP. Record relevant results and the query that produced them. Compare endpoint and identity evidence where available. Escalate with a concise timeline, impact, actions already taken, and the next decision needed.\u003c/p>","question":"How can a SIEM contain logs without producing an alert about those logs?","answer":"Collection and detection are separate. The source may be sending events successfully while no enabled rule matches the behavior, its threshold is not reached, or its query looks in the wrong table or time range. Check the raw events before changing the detection.","sources":["sentinelOverview","sentinelIncident","ama"],"related":["syslog","alert-triage","incident-response"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — What is Microsoft Sentinel?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/sentinel/overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Investigating a Sentinel incident","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/sentinel/incident-investigation"},{"title":"Microsoft — Windows events via AMA","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/sentinel/connect-services-windows-based"}]},{"slug":"ztna-sase","title":"ZTNA / SASE","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"ZTNA checks who is requesting access and the conditions of that request before allowing a path to a resource. SASE combines secure access with wider networking and security services.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with a remote employee\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An employee at home needs a company finance application. That application might be on a private office server, so an ordinary internet connection is not enough. \u003cstrong>Zero Trust Network Access\u003c/strong> provides an approved path based on identity, the requested resource, and configured context such as device status.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A resource can be an internal web application or another supported private service. The employee may use a local client or a browser, depending on the product and application. They do not necessarily open a virtual Windows desktop. The goal is to grant the required access with the conditions the organization has chosen.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Walk through an access request\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>The employee signs in using the organization’s identity provider.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The service evaluates the applicable access policy: user or group, resource, and relevant device or risk conditions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If requirements are met, the system provides the permitted connection to that resource.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The application still checks its own sign-in and permissions. Network access does not automatically grant access to every file.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Access can be reevaluated when supported signals or sessions change. The exact timing and behavior depend on the implementation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Compare the experiences\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Approach\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What the employee experiences\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>VPN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A tunnel provides access to configured network routes. Its reach can be broad or tightly restricted; a VPN does not inherently grant every permission.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ZTNA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Access is granted according to identity, resource, and policy context. Supported protocols and the granularity of controls depend on the product.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Virtual desktop, such as a Citrix desktop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The employee operates a remote desktop or published application. Much of the work runs on the remote system rather than directly on the local laptop.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A personal computer is not universally allowed or forbidden by ZTNA. The organization may require a managed device, allow limited browser access, or use another design. Likewise, “always on” describes how a configured client maintains protection; it does not mean every resource is available to every signed-in user.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Where SASE fits\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Secure Access Service Edge\u003c/strong>, commonly pronounced “sassy,” is the broader architecture. It combines networking, such as software-defined wide area networking (SD-WAN), with cloud-delivered security. Common parts include ZTNA, web filtering through a secure web gateway, controls for cloud application use through a CASB, and firewall-as-a-service. A product may deliver a subset; verify the deployed capabilities.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on a support ticket\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For “I can sign in but cannot reach the app,” separate identity, policy, network path, and application permissions. A successful MFA challenge confirms one requirement, not every requirement. Check the request’s policy result, device state, DNS, connector or gateway health, and the resource itself. A precise denial reason is more useful than turning off a policy to make the error disappear.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Conditional Access is one part of the picture\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A sign-in policy can require MFA or a compliant device for an application. By itself, that policy does not create a network path from a home laptop to a private file server. The access service and the application’s own permissions still matter.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"Why does successful MFA not guarantee that an employee can open a private file share?","answer":"MFA satisfies an authentication requirement. The device and request must also meet the access policy, the private server must be reachable through an approved path, and the user must have permission on the share and files.","sources":["sase","privateAccess","ca"],"related":["vpns","security-controls"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Cisco — SASE and its components","url":"https://www.cisco.com/site/us/en/learn/topics/security/what-is-secure-access-service-edge-sase.html"},{"title":"Microsoft — Entra Private Access","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/global-secure-access/concept-private-access"},{"title":"Microsoft — Conditional Access","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/overview"}]},{"slug":"email-auth","title":"Email authentication","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"SPF, DKIM, and DMARC help check whether a message is authorized to use a domain. They do not prove that the sender is honest or that the request is safe.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with the visible sender\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An email contains several sender-related fields. The display name is the friendly label, such as “Finance Director.” The \u003cstrong>From\u003c/strong> address is what the mail client displays. The \u003cstrong>envelope sender\u003c/strong> is used during delivery and can differ from that visible From address. Authentication checks these identities in specific ways; a convincing display name proves very little.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What each check means\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Check\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Plain-English question\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Limit\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>SPF — Sender Policy Framework\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Is this sending server authorized in DNS to send for the envelope-sender domain?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An SPF pass alone does not authenticate the visible From domain. Forwarding can complicate SPF.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DKIM — DomainKeys Identified Mail\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does a valid signature from the signing domain verify the signed parts of this message?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>It checks signed content and domain responsibility, not the truth of the message or every possible header.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DMARC — Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Did SPF or DKIM pass with a domain that aligns with the visible From domain?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A passing message can still be fraudulent. A failing result needs delivery-path context.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>DMARC needs at least one aligned passing method, not both. \u003cstrong>Alignment\u003c/strong> means the authenticated domain matches the visible From domain according to the configured strict or relaxed rules. Relaxed alignment can allow related subdomains of the same organizational domain.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Compare three messages\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Fictional case\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What the checks can tell you\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Forged From: finance@company.example, sent through an unauthorized system\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DMARC can fail because neither SPF nor DKIM passes with alignment to company.example.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>From: finance@company-payments.example, a lookalike domain owned by an attacker\u003c/td>\u003ctd>SPF, DKIM, and DMARC can all pass for the attacker’s own domain. The domain still is not company.example.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A real employee mailbox has been compromised\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Messages sent through the legitimate service can authenticate successfully while carrying fraudulent instructions.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Read it during phishing review\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Use your email security platform or the original message headers to inspect the receiving system’s authentication results. Compare the actual From address, Reply-To, envelope sender, DKIM signing domain, and links with the organization the sender claims to represent. A raw authentication-looking header supplied by an untrusted sender is not automatically trustworthy; use the results from your trusted receiving system.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Then inspect the request itself. Was a bank account changed? Is the message pressuring the user to bypass a normal process? Did the recipient click, download, enter credentials, or approve MFA? Search for other recipients and related activity through the available email tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Understand the policy\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A domain’s DMARC policy can request monitoring only (\u003ccode>p=none\u003c/code>), quarantine, or rejection of failing mail. Receiver handling also depends on the receiving system’s policies. Publishing a monitoring policy is not the same as enforcing rejection.\u003c/p>","question":"Why can a lookalike domain pass DMARC?","answer":"The attacker can configure valid SPF and DKIM for the domain they own, aligned with that domain’s From address. DMARC checks domain authorization, not whether the domain is the real company or whether the payment request is legitimate.","sources":["spf","dkim","dmarc"],"related":["bec","dns","phishing","smtp","imap"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — SPF email authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-spf-configure"},{"title":"Microsoft — DKIM email authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dkim-configure"},{"title":"Microsoft — DMARC and alignment","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dmarc-configure"}]},{"slug":"bec","title":"Business email compromise","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"Business email compromise is a scam that uses a believable business conversation to trick someone into sending money, sharing information, or changing an important process.","body":"\u003ch2>Recognize the attack\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A message appears to come from a supplier: “Our bank details changed; please use this account.” It may contain no malware and no suspicious attachment. The attacker wants the employee to perform the harmful action willingly. BEC is an attack scenario, not an email security framework.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The message might use a forged sender, a similar-looking domain, or an actual compromised mailbox. Attackers can reply inside an existing conversation and copy its tone. A familiar name, a real signature, and passing email authentication do not establish that a new payment instruction is legitimate.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Start with the requested action and exposure\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Ask the recipient to pause the requested action and avoid further interaction with the suspicious message.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Determine what already happened: reply, click, download, credential entry, MFA approval, data disclosure, or payment.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Preserve the original message and useful identifiers, headers, timestamps, and relevant conversation history.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Verify the business request through a known independent contact method, such as a number already in approved supplier records. Do not use new contact details supplied by the suspect message.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Investigate more than the header\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Inspect the sender domains, Reply-To, authentication results, links, and message delivery. Search for other recipients and related messages. If a mailbox may be compromised, examine sign-ins, mailbox rules, forwarding, sent messages, permission changes, and other relevant audit activity with the tools available. Distinguish what is observed from what is still suspected.\u003c/p>\u003cp>SPF, DKIM, and DMARC help evaluate domain use. They do not clear a legitimate but compromised account or a well-configured lookalike domain. The authenticity of a payment change still needs independent business verification.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Match the response to what happened\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Exposure\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Typical response work within the incident plan\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Message received, no interaction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Evaluate and scope the message; remove or quarantine confirmed malicious copies as authorized and explain the finding to the user.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Credentials entered or suspicious MFA approved\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Coordinate account containment, credential and session response, authentication-method review, and investigation of account activity.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Attachment executed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Investigate the endpoint and consider immediate containment using the available evidence and authority.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Payment or sensitive data already sent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Escalate immediately through the organization’s incident and business response process. For payment fraud, the responsible team should promptly contact its financial institution.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Make the handoff useful\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Record the claimed sender, actual domain, recipients, actions the user took, evidence, affected accounts or devices, containment already completed, and the owner of each next step. A high-impact event may need containment and escalation while further confirmation continues. Avoid a rule such as “always wait until completely certain” that could delay an urgent response.\u003c/p>","question":"A supplier’s email passes DMARC and asks for new bank details. Is it safe to approve the change?","answer":"No. A compromised supplier mailbox or a lookalike domain can pass authentication. Pause the change, verify it through an existing trusted contact method, and investigate the message and any user interaction. Escalate promptly if money or sensitive information has already been sent.","sources":["bec","dmarc","compromised"],"related":["email-auth","identity","phishing","incident-response","smtp","imap","pop3"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"FBI — Business email compromise","url":"https://www.fbi.gov/how-we-can-help-you/common-frauds-and-scams/business-email-compromise"},{"title":"Microsoft — DMARC and alignment","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dmarc-configure"},{"title":"Microsoft — Respond to a compromised email account","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/responding-to-a-compromised-email-account"}]},{"slug":"identity","title":"Risky sign-ins","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Scenario study","principle":"A risky sign-in is an authentication event with signals that deserve investigation. The signal helps prioritize work; the surrounding evidence determines whether an account is compromised.","body":"\u003ch2>Understand the signal\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An account signs in from Colorado and soon afterward appears to sign in from another continent. That travel seems implausible, so an identity system may flag it. An \u003cstrong>impossible-travel\u003c/strong> style detection compares time and apparent locations; other signals include unfamiliar properties or known malicious infrastructure. Names and logic vary by provider.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A network location is normally inferred from the IP address. A VPN, mobile provider, cloud proxy, or inaccurate location database can change that appearance without the user physically moving. Conversely, a familiar location is not proof of safety. Attackers may use nearby infrastructure or an already-established session.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read the sign-in as a record\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Field\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Why it matters\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>User and tenant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Identify the actual account and organization. Similar display names can mislead.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Time and result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Establish whether authentication succeeded and keep the time zone consistent.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Application / resource\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Identify what the account was trying to access.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>IP, device, client, and location\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compare with the account’s usual pattern and known company infrastructure.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Authentication details\u003c/td>\u003ctd>See how MFA or another requirement was satisfied; a success can rely on an earlier claim or session.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Conditional Access result\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Identify which policies applied, which requirement failed, or why access was allowed.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Work through the investigation\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Confirm the alert’s evidence and whether suspicious access succeeded. Compare nearby sign-ins, devices, and expected VPN use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Contact the user through a trusted channel to verify relevant activity, without relying only on their recollection.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Look for subsequent resource activity: mailbox rules, sent messages, downloaded files, changed authentication methods, or new permissions where logs are available.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Assess impact and urgency. Use the incident plan to contain credible active compromise while continuing investigation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Document the evidence, alternative explanations, actions, and the remaining uncertainty.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Containment is more than a password reset\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Authorized response can include blocking sign-in, resetting credentials, revoking sessions, and reviewing registered authentication methods or suspicious application access. These actions have different effects. Revocation is not a promise that every existing token or application session immediately becomes unusable; token lifetime, application behavior, and supported continuous evaluation matter. Verify the observed outcome.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Do not delete every authentication method automatically because a location looked unusual. Determine what was changed or abused and use the approved recovery procedure. Similarly, changing a password does not remove a malicious forwarding rule or identify which data was accessed.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use the right source of evidence\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Entra sign-in logs explain authentication. Email and cloud application audit records show relevant activity within those services. Endpoint tools show device activity. Intune provides device-management and compliance information; it is not a universal record of everything an attacker did.\u003c/p>","question":"Two successful sign-ins appear far apart within minutes. What would make you more or less concerned?","answer":"Compare known VPN or proxy use, device and client details, authentication and policy results, and subsequent activity. A user-confirmed VPN with expected behavior may explain the location shift. Unexpected MFA changes, mailbox forwarding, or unfamiliar resource access increase concern and may justify prompt containment under the response plan.","sources":["risk","ca","compromised"],"related":["bec","alert-triage","security-controls"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Identity risk detections","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-protection/concept-identity-protection-risks"},{"title":"Microsoft — Conditional Access","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/conditional-access/overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Respond to a compromised email account","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/responding-to-a-compromised-email-account"}]},{"slug":"windows-domain","title":"Active Directory","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Active Directory Domain Services keeps a shared directory of users, computers, and groups. Domain controllers use it to support domain authentication and centralized administration.","body":"\u003ch2>Server, role, and domain controller\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Windows Server\u003c/strong> is an operating system. \u003cstrong>Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS)\u003c/strong> is a role it can run. A server promoted to run AD DS as a domain controller maintains the directory and provides domain services. A file server can run Windows Server without being a domain controller.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>domain\u003c/strong> is a directory and administration boundary containing objects such as user accounts and computers. A \u003cstrong>group\u003c/strong> collects identities so permissions can be assigned together. An \u003cstrong>organizational unit (OU)\u003c/strong> is a container used to organize objects, delegate administration, and apply Group Policy. An OU is not a permission group.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Follow an ordinary domain sign-in\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>A domain-joined PC has a relationship with the domain. It uses DNS to locate appropriate domain services.\u003c/li>\u003cli>During a typical connected domain sign-in, Windows communicates with a domain controller to authenticate the user. Kerberos is commonly used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Kerberos provides tickets the user can use to request access to supported services without sending the password to every server.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The destination service checks authorization, including group membership and its own permissions. A successful logon does not grant access to all files.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Windows can permit cached interactive sign-in when a domain controller is unavailable, depending on configuration and prior sign-ins. Reaching the desktop therefore does not prove the PC can currently contact a domain controller or obtain access to a network resource.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What Group Policy does\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Administrators use Group Policy Objects (GPOs) to apply settings to users and computers, such as password-related configuration, firewall settings, or drive mappings. Where a GPO is linked, its filtering, inheritance, and processing determine who receives it. Changing a domain-wide GPO can affect many systems.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Troubleshoot from the symptom\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Useful checks\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One user cannot sign in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact error, correct domain, account enabled or locked, password status, and relevant log events.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many devices cannot use domain services\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DNS configuration, domain controller reachability, service health, and recent changes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Desktop sign-in works but a share fails\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Network path, current domain connectivity, user identity, group membership, and share/file permissions.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Expected policy is missing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Applied-policy results, object location, links and filtering, and domain connectivity.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Keep AD DS and Entra ID distinct\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Microsoft Entra ID, formerly Azure AD, is Microsoft’s cloud identity service. It is not simply an on-premises domain controller with a new name. Organizations can synchronize selected identities between AD DS and Entra ID, so a user may appear in both. Identify whether the problem concerns a local account, domain account, cloud sign-in, or synchronization before choosing a console or reset procedure.\u003c/p>","question":"Does every Windows Server machine validate domain logons?","answer":"No. Windows Server can host many roles. Domain controllers running AD DS provide domain authentication services. A server used only for file sharing or an application is not automatically a domain controller.","sources":["ad","kerberos","adCompare"],"related":["file-shares","security-controls","ldap","rpc-netbios","smb"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Active Directory Domain Services overview","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/active-directory-domain-services-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Kerberos authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/kerberos/kerberos-authentication-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Compare directory services","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/domain-services/compare-identity-solutions"}]},{"slug":"file-shares","title":"File shares","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A file share lets other computers access a folder over the network. The network path finds the server and share; the user’s permissions decide what they can do there.","body":"\u003ch2>Read the path\u003c/h2>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>\\\\files-01\\Finance\nZ:  →  \\\\files-01\\Finance\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>A path beginning with two backslashes is a \u003cstrong>UNC path\u003c/strong>. \u003ccode>files-01\u003c/code> is the server name and \u003ccode>Finance\u003c/code> is the share name. A mapped drive such as \u003ccode>Z:\u003c/code> is a convenient local shortcut to that path. Losing the drive letter does not necessarily mean the files or server disappeared.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A file server can be a general-purpose server offering folders. A \u003cstrong>NAS\u003c/strong>, or network-attached storage device, is a storage-focused appliance that can also offer shares. Both may provide SMB, the file-sharing protocol commonly used by Windows. Modern direct SMB commonly uses TCP port 445.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Four things must work\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Name:\u003c/strong> the computer resolves the server name to the intended address.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Path:\u003c/strong> the network and any approved remote-access service can reach the server’s SMB service.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Identity:\u003c/strong> the server authenticates the account being used.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Permission:\u003c/strong> the account is authorized to use the share and the requested files.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Being on the same network or connected through a VPN does not expose every person’s files automatically. A folder must be shared and permissions must allow the requested access. The same applies when an access service permits the network connection.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Share permissions and file permissions\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Windows file access over a share is constrained by both the share’s permissions and the file system’s permissions, commonly NTFS permissions. Allowing Modify on the share does not override a file permission that prevents the write. Use effective-access tools and the actual group memberships when troubleshooting; inherited permissions and deny entries can make a quick glance misleading.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Troubleshoot a missing mapped drive\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Record the drive letter, expected UNC path, user, error, and whether other users are affected.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Try the known UNC path. If it works, investigate the mapping or its deployment policy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the name fails, check DNS and the remote-access path. A service-port test can help separate reachability from authorization.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the share opens but writing fails, check the exact account, share permissions, file permissions, and storage state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If everyone is affected, inspect server and service health, disk space, and recent changes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Resolve-DnsName files-01\nTest-NetConnection files-01 -Port 445\nGet-SmbMapping\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>These Windows checks inspect name resolution, a TCP connection, and existing drive mappings. Substitute the actual server. A successful TCP check does not prove the user can authenticate or read a folder. Avoid granting broad access as a shortcut; restore the intended access for the affected group.\u003c/p>","question":"Z: disappeared, but the expected UNC path opens successfully. What is your next focus?","answer":"The server path and this user’s basic access work. Investigate the drive mapping, sign-in script, Group Policy, or device-management configuration that should create Z:. Also check whether the problem is limited to one session or device.","sources":["smb","dns","kerberos"],"related":["windows-domain","vpns","smb","rpc-netbios"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — SMB file sharing","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/file-server-smb-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — Troubleshooting DNS clients","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/dns/troubleshoot/troubleshoot-dns-client"},{"title":"Microsoft — Kerberos authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/kerberos/kerberos-authentication-overview"}]},{"slug":"windows-events","title":"Windows events","category":"Cloud & telemetry","kind":"Concept","basis":"Lab application","principle":"Windows event logs record activity on a computer. Event IDs tell you what kind of event was recorded; the event’s fields explain who, where, when, and how.","body":"\u003ch2>Find the event at its source\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Open Event Viewer on the intended Windows machine and inspect the relevant log. \u003cstrong>System\u003c/strong> includes operating-system and service events. \u003cstrong>Application\u003c/strong> contains application events. \u003cstrong>Security\u003c/strong> contains audited security activity. Which events appear depends on audit configuration, system role, activity, and log retention.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Filter by a sensible time range and event ID, then open the event’s details. Note the computer name and time zone. A log collector provides another copy or view of selected data; finding an event locally helps determine whether an apparent gap is a collection problem.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Three sign-in events to recognize\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Event ID\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it records\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it does not prove\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4624\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A successful logon on the computer recording it.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That a person opened a desktop, or that the access was authorized.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4625\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A failed logon attempt.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That an attacker succeeded or that every failure was malicious.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>4672\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sensitive privileges assigned to a new logon.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A fresh privilege-escalation attack; legitimate administrative and system logons can generate it.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Read the logon type\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A logon is not always someone typing into the Windows sign-in screen. \u003cstrong>Type 2\u003c/strong> is interactive; \u003cstrong>type 3\u003c/strong> is network, such as access to a shared resource; \u003cstrong>type 5\u003c/strong> is a service; \u003cstrong>type 10\u003c/strong> is RemoteInteractive, commonly RDP. There are additional types, so check the event reference for unfamiliar values.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A type 3 logon does not mean an RDP desktop session. Also, the “Subject” account can be the account reporting or requesting the operation. Read the relevant New Logon or Account For Which Logon Failed fields rather than treating every account field as the same identity.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Build a small timeline\u003c/h2>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Fictional example on SRV-01\n10:01  4625  user: alex  source: 10.20.10.25\n10:02  4625  user: alex  source: 10.20.10.25\n10:03  4624  user: alex  logon type: 10  logon ID: 0x8F12\n10:03  4672  subject: alex  logon ID: 0x8F12\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>This is a sequence worth checking, but two failed passwords followed by a legitimate admin logon is one possible explanation. Match the computer and Logon ID to associate relevant events within that host session, alongside timestamps and account details. Logon IDs are not a global identifier across every machine or reboot. Then inspect what the session did and whether the source and account use were expected.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use the fields to avoid overclaiming\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For failed logons, inspect the status and substatus, logon type, account, and source information. For successes, determine the session type and related activity. A missing or placeholder IP does not identify a remote source. Record “successful authentication observed” separately from “compromise confirmed.”\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>Local first, then collection\u003c/strong>\u003cp>If Event Viewer contains a known event but the query platform does not, check collection rules, agent health, table, workspace, and time range. If neither contains it, check whether that event was generated and auditing was enabled.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"A 4624 is followed by a 4672 with the same Logon ID on one host. Does that prove privilege escalation?","answer":"No. It shows a successful logon and sensitive privileges assigned to that session. This can be normal for an administrative or system account. Check the account, logon type, source, time, expected role, and subsequent actions to decide whether the activity is suspicious.","sources":["e4624","e4625","e4672","eventViewer"],"related":["windows-domain","alert-triage"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Successful logon: 4624","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4624"},{"title":"Microsoft — Failed logon: 4625","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4625"},{"title":"Microsoft — Special privileges: 4672","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/it-pro/windows-10/security/threat-protection/auditing/event-4672"},{"title":"Microsoft — Get-WinEvent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent"}]},{"slug":"tls","title":"TLS","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"TLS protects data in transit by encrypting it and checking that it has not been changed. In a normal HTTPS connection, it also helps the browser verify the server’s identity.","body":"\u003ch2>Put it in a web connection\u003c/h2>\u003cp>When you use \u003ccode>https://\u003c/code>, the browser uses HTTP with TLS protection. \u003cstrong>HTTP\u003c/strong> describes the web request and response. \u003cstrong>TLS\u003c/strong>, Transport Layer Security, protects the exchanged data. \u003cstrong>TCP\u003c/strong> commonly provides reliable delivery for HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, while IP carries packets between network addresses. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP and incorporates TLS 1.3.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Plain HTTP commonly uses TCP too. TCP gives ordered delivery and recovery from loss; it does not encrypt the application’s contents. Adding encryption is not the same as changing whether traffic uses IP.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What happens during the handshake?\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>The client and server agree on supported TLS parameters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The server presents a certificate. The client checks the requested hostname, validity, and trust chain under its validation rules.\u003c/li>\u003cli>They establish shared session keys using the negotiated handshake.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Application data then travels in protected records that provide confidentiality and integrity.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>A certificate links an identity, commonly a hostname, to a public key through a trust system. It is not a promise that the business is honest. A malicious website can have a valid certificate for its own domain.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Keep the security promises precise\u003c/h2>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Property\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Confidentiality\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A passive observer should not be able to read protected application contents.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Integrity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tampering with protected records should be detected.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Authentication\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The client verifies the server’s identity through certificate validation in a typical HTTPS connection. Client certificates are optional in ordinary web use.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Limits\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TLS does not stop a compromised endpoint from reading its own plaintext or make a harmful website safe. Some traffic metadata remains visible.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch2>Versions and certificates\u003c/h2>\u003cp>TLS 1.3 is the modern protocol version. TLS 1.2 can still be secure with appropriate algorithms and configuration; it is not automatically broken just because 1.3 exists. Older SSL and TLS versions should not be used as a workaround. A failed certificate check can indicate an expired certificate, wrong hostname, untrusted issuer, incorrect system time, or authorized or unauthorized interception.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it on an application ticket\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A service can answer on TCP 443 while TLS or the application still fails. Record the exact browser or client error and affected hostname. Check the certificate’s subject names, dates, trust chain, and the device’s clock. Compare another device and network path, including any proxy or security inspection. Do not teach the user to ignore certificate warnings as a routine fix.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>One boundary at a time\u003c/strong>\u003cp>A successful DNS lookup finds the address. A TCP test checks transport reachability. A successful TLS handshake checks the negotiated protected channel. The application still has to respond correctly.\u003c/p>\u003c/aside>","question":"A site has a valid HTTPS certificate. Does that mean the site’s content is trustworthy?","answer":"No. A valid certificate helps authenticate the connection to that hostname and protect data in transit. An attacker can obtain a valid certificate for a domain they control, and a legitimate site can also be compromised.","sources":["tls","tcp","http3"],"related":["tcp","dns","firewalls","osi","http","smtp","ldap"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 9325 — Secure use of TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9325.html"},{"title":"RFC 9293 — TCP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html"},{"title":"RFC 9114 — HTTP/3","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114"}]},{"slug":"tcp","title":"TCP","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"TCP carries an ordered stream of bytes between two endpoints. It tracks delivery and retransmits missing data so applications do not have to handle ordinary packet loss themselves.","body":"\u003ch2>Identify the two ends\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A TCP connection has a source IP and port and a destination IP and port. For example, a laptop at \u003ccode>10.0.0.25:53010\u003c/code> might connect to a web service at \u003ccode>192.0.2.80:443\u003c/code>. The client commonly chooses a temporary, or \u003cstrong>ephemeral\u003c/strong>, source port. Replies reverse the source and destination. The documentation address here represents a fictional server.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two connections to the same server can use different client ports. A browser tab does not map neatly to one TCP connection: requests may share a connection or use several. HTTP/2 can carry multiple request streams over one TCP connection.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Open, transfer, and close\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK:\u003c/strong> the usual three-way handshake establishes a connection and synchronizes initial sequence numbers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Data and acknowledgements:\u003c/strong> the sender transmits bytes and the receiver indicates what it has received.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retransmission:\u003c/strong> missing data can be sent again. Flow control limits data to what the receiver can handle; congestion control responds to network conditions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>FIN / ACK or RST:\u003c/strong> a connection can close in an orderly way or be reset. Interpret the surrounding packets before deciding why.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch2>Sequence number versus conversation number\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A TCP \u003cstrong>sequence number\u003c/strong> identifies a position in the byte stream. If a segment starts at sequence 1000 and carries 100 data bytes, the next data byte would normally be sequence 1100. An acknowledgement generally identifies the next byte expected. SYN and FIN each consume sequence-number space too.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Wireshark’s \u003ccode>tcp.stream\u003c/code> is different: it is an analysis index identifying a conversation within a capture. It is not transmitted in the TCP header. The stream index helps you select a conversation; sequence numbers help you follow the bytes inside it.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Why the header length is a multiple of four\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A TCP header starts with a fixed 20-byte part. Optional fields can make it longer. Its \u003cstrong>Data Offset\u003c/strong> field stores the total header length in units of four bytes, called 32-bit words. A value of 5 means 20 bytes; 8 means 32 bytes. The receiver uses that length to find where application data begins.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"tcp\" aria-label=\"Find where the payload begins\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Find where the payload begins\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the number of option bytes. See how padding makes the total TCP header fit the four-byte units used by Data Offset.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Enable JavaScript to use this exercise. The worked examples in this note cover the same ideas.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\u003cp>A No-Operation option is one byte and can align options. End-of-option-list padding can also fill remaining header space. The “packing peanuts” analogy is about alignment, not adding application data. For a simple 20-byte base plus 10 bytes of options, two more bytes make a 32-byte header.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Use it during troubleshooting\u003c/h2>\u003cp>No reply to SYN can indicate filtering, routing, an unavailable host, or capture limitations. A reset can indicate refusal or a closed connection, but inspect its source and timing. Repeated retransmissions can suggest loss or missing capture visibility. TCP itself does not provide encryption; TLS supplies protection for many applications.\u003c/p>","question":"A TCP segment starts at sequence 1000 and carries 100 data bytes. Is tcp.stream == 1100 the way to find the next bytes?","answer":"No. Sequence 1100 would describe the next data-byte position in this simplified example. tcp.stream is Wireshark’s separate identifier for the whole connection. Filter the right stream, then inspect sequence and acknowledgement numbers inside it.","sources":["tcp","tcpGuide","wireshark"],"related":["tls","firewalls","routing","osi","ftp","http","ssh"],"keywords":"","references":[{"title":"RFC 9293 — TCP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html"},{"title":"Wireshark — TCP analysis","url":"https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChAdvTCPAnalysis.html"},{"title":"Wireshark — TCP display fields","url":"https://www.wireshark.org/docs/dfref/t/tcp.html"}]},{"slug":"ftp","title":"FTP","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"FTP transfers files using one connection for instructions and another for the actual data. That separation explains many FTP connection problems.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>File Transfer Protocol (FTP)\u003c/strong> lets a client list, upload, and download files on a server. Think of a receptionist taking your request while a separate delivery route carries the package. Logging in successfully only proves that the conversation with the receptionist worked.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>FTP defines application commands such as listing a directory and retrieving a file. It does not forward frames or route IP packets. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003ch2>Two connections, two jobs\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>control connection\u003c/strong> carries login information, commands, and replies. A separate \u003cstrong>data connection\u003c/strong> carries file contents or a directory listing. Uploads and downloads both use the data connection; port 20 does not mean “download” and port 21 does not mean “upload.”\u003c/p>\u003cp>In \u003cstrong>active mode\u003c/strong>, the server starts the data connection back to a port supplied by the client. In \u003cstrong>passive mode\u003c/strong>, the client starts it toward a port advertised by the server. This changes which direction a firewall must permit. Passive mode is often easier for clients behind NAT.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">FTP control\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 21\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Commands, login, and replies.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Active FTP data\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Typically server TCP 20 → client\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The client supplies its receiving port.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Passive FTP data\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Negotiated server TCP port\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usually drawn from a configured range.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">FTPS\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 21 explicit / 990 implicit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>FTP with TLS; data connections still need protection.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">SFTP\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 22\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A different protocol carried over SSH.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"ftp-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"ftp-lab-title\">Why can login work but files fail?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional supplier transfer uses passive FTP. All ports below are illustrative. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ftp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Only port 21 is allowed\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Will the directory listing load?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client signs in\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Control connection to TCP 21 succeeds.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Server offers 50050\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client tries this separate data port.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Listing stalls\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The example firewall blocks TCP 50050.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The login and data paths have different outcomes. A successful login does not prove that a file can move.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the negotiated port, the configured passive range, NAT address, and the matching firewall logs before changing a rule.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ftp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The required data path is allowed\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What changes after the scoped fix?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Control succeeds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client requests a directory listing.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Data connects\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Client reaches the advertised TCP 50050.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Listing arrives\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The server sends the directory data.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Both connections now work in this example. The same data-path principle applies to file transfers.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Confirm the actual transfer result and permissions. Plain FTP remains unencrypted even though connectivity works.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ftp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Switch the client to SFTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Can it reuse the FTP listener?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client chooses SFTP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It expects an SSH service, commonly on TCP 22.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>FTP server listens\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>An FTP-only listener speaks a different protocol.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>No compatible service\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Changing the label does not convert the server.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>SFTP is SSH file transfer. FTPS is FTP protected with TLS. Their similar names do not make them interchangeable.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Confirm which service the supplier actually offers; choose matching client settings and an approved secure method.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Security and a practical support check\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Ordinary FTP exposes credentials and data to someone who can observe the connection. \u003cstrong>FTPS\u003c/strong> adds \u003ca href=\"#note-tls\">TLS\u003c/a>; require protection of both control and data traffic. Explicit FTPS requests a TLS upgrade on the control connection, commonly TCP 21. Implicit FTPS expects TLS immediately, commonly on TCP 990. \u003cstrong>SFTP\u003c/strong> uses \u003ca href=\"#note-ssh\">SSH\u003c/a> instead of FTP’s two-connection design. A secure protocol protects the transfer, but it cannot make an untrusted file harmless.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a failed scheduled transfer, record the server, mode, exact reply, time, and last successful step. A timeout, a rejected login, and a file-permission error need different investigations. Avoid opening a huge port range simply because “FTP is broken.”\u003c/p>","question":"Why might an FTP login succeed while a directory listing times out?","answer":"The login uses the control connection, commonly TCP 21. Listings use a separate data connection. Check active/passive mode and the actual negotiated data path, then verify the firewall, NAT, and server configuration.","sources":["ftpProtocol","ftpPassive","ftpTLS","sftpManual","servicePorts"],"related":["tcp","tls","ssh","firewalls","private-addressing","osi"],"keywords":"FTP FTPS SFTP file transfer active passive TCP 20 21 22 990 control data ports","references":[{"title":"RFC 959 — File Transfer Protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc959/"},{"title":"RFC 2428 — Extended passive and active FTP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc2428/"},{"title":"RFC 4217 — Securing FTP with TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc4217/"},{"title":"OpenBSD — SFTP manual","url":"https://man.openbsd.org/sftp"},{"title":"IANA: service names and transport protocol port numbers","url":"https://www.iana.org/assignments/service-names-port-numbers/service-names-port-numbers.xhtml"}]},{"slug":"telnet","title":"Telnet","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Telnet provides a remote text terminal. Ordinary Telnet does not encrypt the session, so SSH is the usual secure choice for remote administration.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Telnet\u003c/strong> lets you type commands into another computer or network device and receive text output. For example, an older switch might present a login prompt over Telnet. The commands run on the remote device, not on your own laptop.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Telnet defines how a remote terminal exchanges text and terminal-control information. Using a TCP port does not make Telnet a Layer 4 protocol. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Telnet\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 23\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Remote terminal; ordinary sessions are plaintext.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">SSH comparison\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 22\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Encrypted remote terminal and related services.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>What plaintext actually means\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Plaintext\u003c/strong> means the protocol does not hide the transmitted content with encryption. Someone positioned to capture the traffic may be able to read usernames, passwords, commands, and responses. A password prompt can authenticate a user while still exposing the password on the network. Authentication and encryption solve different problems.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The terminal window itself tells you little about security. \u003ca href=\"#note-ssh\">SSH\u003c/a> can look almost identical while protecting the connection. Moving Telnet to an unusual port changes where it listens; that alone adds no encryption.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"telnet-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"telnet-lab-title\">Same terminal, different protection\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional technician needs to inspect a legacy switch. No real credentials or network traffic are used. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-telnet\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Use ordinary Telnet\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What could an observer on the path read?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Technician types\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>A made-up login and command enter the client.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>TCP 23 carries text\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The session has no protocol encryption.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Switch responds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The output can also be readable on the path.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The username, password, and commands may be exposed. Being inside an office network does not itself encrypt them.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Document the legacy dependency and arrange an approved migration or restricted temporary access path.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-telnet\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Use SSH with verified identity\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What changes on the wire?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Verify the host\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The known server identity is checked.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Encrypted session\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>SSH protects authentication and command traffic.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Switch responds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The authorized client decrypts the output.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The text interface looks familiar, but the communication is encrypted and integrity-protected.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The account still needs appropriate rights. Encryption does not make every command or logged-in user trustworthy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-telnet\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>A port test succeeds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Does this prove the remote login works?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>TCP connects\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The target accepts a connection at the tested port.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>No login tested\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The result contains no authentication evidence.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Access remains unknown\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Command execution and permissions were not checked.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>A successful connection is a limited reachability result. It does not establish the application, encryption, or user access.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Record “TCP connection succeeded,” then use the approved application check for the actual service.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Where this matters at work\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A vulnerability report may flag Telnet on a printer, switch, or appliance. First establish which asset owns the address and whether the service is needed. Check the supported secure-management option, who can reach the interface, and the change owner. Do not disable a production management path before confirming a working replacement.\u003c/p>\u003cp>You may also hear “telnet to a port” as an old connectivity test. That refers to using a Telnet client against a chosen port; it does not mean the service on that port is Telnet. An HTTPS service, for example, expects a TLS handshake rather than ordinary typed text. Troubleshooting should separate basic reachability from a working application.\u003c/p>","question":"Does moving Telnet from port 23 to a different port protect the password?","answer":"No. The port changes the listening endpoint, not the protocol’s encryption. Ordinary Telnet remains plaintext. Use an approved encrypted management service such as SSH and verify the server identity.","sources":["telnetProtocol","sshProtocol","sshManual","connectionTest"],"related":["ssh","tcp","security-controls","osi"],"keywords":"Telnet TCP 23 remote terminal plaintext cleartext SSH port testing legacy management","references":[{"title":"RFC 854 — Telnet protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc854/"},{"title":"RFC 4251 — Secure Shell architecture","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc4251/"},{"title":"OpenBSD — SSH client manual","url":"https://man.openbsd.org/ssh"},{"title":"Microsoft — Test-NetConnection","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/test-netconnection"}]},{"slug":"imap","title":"IMAP / IMAPS","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"IMAP lets a mail client work with messages and folders stored on a server. IMAPS protects that same mailbox conversation with TLS from the start.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Internet Message Access Protocol (IMAP)\u003c/strong> is a way to read and organize a server mailbox. Imagine your laptop and phone looking at the same filing cabinet. Each device may keep a local copy, but the server stores the shared mailbox state, such as folders and read flags.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>IMAP defines mailbox operations such as selecting a folder, fetching a message, and changing a read flag. These are application tasks, not IP routing. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">IMAP\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 143\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can upgrade to TLS using STARTTLS.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">IMAPS\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 993\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TLS starts immediately, before IMAP commands.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Syncing is different from sending\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An IMAP client can fetch a message, mark it read, move it between folders, or request deletion. Other connected clients can learn about the updated server state. An offline device may still show old information until it reconnects and synchronizes. Local caching makes offline reading possible; it is not a separate backup policy.\u003c/p>\u003cp>IMAP does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> submit outgoing mail. \u003ca href=\"#note-smtp\">SMTP\u003c/a> handles sending and relaying. \u003ca href=\"#note-pop3\">POP3\u003c/a> is another way to retrieve messages, but it does not provide IMAP’s shared folder and flag model. Some mail apps use HTTPS-based services instead, so do not assume every phone or Outlook connection uses IMAP.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"imap-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"imap-lab-title\">One mailbox, two devices\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional employee reads a message on a laptop while a phone uses the same IMAP mailbox. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-imap\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Both devices are online\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Will the phone learn that the message was read?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Laptop marks read\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It updates the message’s read flag on the server.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Mailbox saves the flag\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The message stays in the shared mailbox.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Phone synchronizes\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The phone displays the updated read state.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>IMAP shares mailbox state through the server. The change is not a direct laptop-to-phone message.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If the phone stays stale, check its connection and synchronization before concluding that the server lost the message.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-imap\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The phone is offline\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Why does it still show “unread”?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Laptop updates server\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The read flag is saved successfully.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Phone has a cached copy\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It cannot receive the new state while offline.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Reconnect and sync\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The phone can then learn the updated flag.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>A stale local display can coexist with a correct server mailbox.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compare webmail or another known-working view and check the client’s last sync time. Avoid deleting local data as the first step.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-imap\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>TLS succeeds, login fails\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Did opening TCP 993 fix the whole problem?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Connection reaches server\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>TCP 993 is reachable.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>TLS completes\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The encrypted channel is established.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Authentication rejected\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The mailbox session is not authorized.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Connectivity, encryption, and authentication are separate checks. The encrypted connection does not grant mailbox access.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the account, approved sign-in method, provider policy, and exact error. Do not switch to plaintext to work around a login failure.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Secure access and investigation clues\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>STARTTLS\u003c/strong> begins with an ordinary protocol greeting, then upgrades the connection before protected login and mailbox work. \u003cstrong>Implicit TLS\u003c/strong>, used by IMAPS on 993, begins with TLS immediately. Require encryption and certificate validation; the port alone does not prove either check succeeded.\u003c/p>\u003cp>During a suspicious-mailbox review, useful facts include the account, source address, client, time, authentication result, and mailbox activity. Successful access alone does not establish which messages were read or stolen. Conversely, TLS only protects the connection: it does not make phishing safe or prevent misuse of a compromised account. Connect this with \u003ca href=\"#note-bec\">business email compromise\u003c/a> and evidence-based \u003ca href=\"#note-alert-triage\">alert triage\u003c/a>.\u003c/p>","question":"A laptop sends mail successfully, but its inbox stops updating. Does that prove SMTP is broken?","answer":"No. Sending and mailbox access are separate paths. If this client uses SMTP to send and IMAP to read, investigate its IMAP connection, TLS, authentication, and synchronization state.","sources":["imapProtocol","mailTLS","smtpSubmission","compromised"],"related":["smtp","pop3","email-auth","bec","tls","osi"],"keywords":"IMAP IMAPS TCP 143 993 STARTTLS email mailbox sync read flags folders","references":[{"title":"RFC 9051 — IMAP4rev2","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9051/"},{"title":"RFC 8314: TLS for email submission and access","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8314"},{"title":"RFC 6409 — Message submission","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc6409/"},{"title":"Microsoft — Respond to a compromised email account","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/responding-to-a-compromised-email-account"}]},{"slug":"pop3","title":"POP3 / POP3S","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"POP3 retrieves messages from a mailbox, commonly into a local mail client. Whether the server keeps a copy depends on the client’s deletion settings.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Post Office Protocol version 3 (POP3)\u003c/strong> lets a client collect mail from a server. Picture picking up letters and keeping them in a folder on your computer. A client may leave a copy at the server, or request removal after download; POP3 does not always delete messages automatically.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>POP3 defines application operations for listing, retrieving, and marking mailbox messages for deletion. TCP carries those requests; POP3 gives them their meaning. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">POP3\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 110\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can upgrade to TLS with the STLS command.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">POP3S\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 995\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TLS begins immediately, before mailbox commands.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Where did the message go?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A POP3 session signs in, works with the mailbox, and ends. \u003ccode>RETR\u003c/code> retrieves a message. \u003ccode>DELE\u003c/code> marks a server message for deletion. After the client issues \u003ccode>QUIT\u003c/code> from the working session, the server enters its update phase and removes marked messages if processing succeeds. An interrupted session before that phase must not perform those requested deletions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The practical question is therefore “What is this client configured to retain?” A setting to leave messages on the server produces different results from a setting to remove them. Unlike \u003ca href=\"#note-imap\">IMAP\u003c/a>, POP3 does not synchronize a full shared folder structure or read/unread state across devices. A second device may download its own copy without knowing what you did to the first.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"pop3-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"pop3-lab-title\">Follow one message\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional mailbox contains one message. The laptop downloads it successfully; no other client changes the mailbox. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-pop3\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Leave a copy on the server\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Where does the message remain?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Server starts with 1\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client retrieves the message.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Laptop stores 1 copy\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It does not request server deletion.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Server still has 1\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Another client can retrieve it too.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Downloading alone does not require deletion. Both locations can now contain a copy.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A local read flag or filing change is not automatically shared with the other device through POP3.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-pop3\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Delete after downloading\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What happens after a successful session end?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Laptop stores 1 copy\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The download completes.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client marks deletion\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It then sends QUIT from the working session.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Server removes the message\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The update succeeds; the laptop copy remains.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>In this scenario, the server mailbox is empty while the laptop still holds the message.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the client’s retention setting before calling missing webmail messages a server outage. Protect local mail with the agreed backup process.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-pop3\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Disconnect before QUIT\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Is the marked server message already deleted?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Message is downloaded\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The local copy is saved.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Deletion is only marked\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The connection drops before QUIT.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Server retains the message\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The deletion update did not occur.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The protocol separates marking a message from completing its removal. The server copy should still exist in this example.\u003c/p>\u003cp>On reconnection, the client’s message tracking affects whether it downloads another copy. Record the interrupted session rather than guessing at data loss.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Secure access and support work\u003c/h2>\u003cp>POP3S provides encrypted transport through \u003ca href=\"#note-tls\">TLS\u003c/a>. POP3 on 110 can also be protected when the STLS upgrade is required and validated before authentication. The secure connection does not turn downloaded mail into a backup or synchronize it across devices.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For “mail appears on only one computer,” compare the account protocol, server mailbox, local folders, retention settings, and last successful collection. Preserve the local mail before rebuilding a profile. POP3 retrieves mail; \u003ca href=\"#note-smtp\">SMTP\u003c/a> is still the separate sending path. A working POP3 download does not prove outgoing mail can be submitted.\u003c/p>","question":"Does POP3 always remove messages from the server after downloading them?","answer":"No. A client can leave copies. Removal depends on deletion requests and a successful update at session end. POP3 does not provide IMAP-style shared folder and read-state synchronization.","sources":["popProtocol","popTLS","mailTLS","imapProtocol"],"related":["imap","smtp","tls","osi"],"keywords":"POP3 POP3S POP 110 995 STLS leave on server download delete QUIT email retrieval","references":[{"title":"RFC 1939 — Post Office Protocol version 3","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc1939/"},{"title":"RFC 2595 — TLS with IMAP and POP3","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc2595/"},{"title":"RFC 8314: TLS for email submission and access","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8314"},{"title":"RFC 9051 — IMAP4rev2","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9051/"}]},{"slug":"snmp","title":"SNMP","category":"Cloud & telemetry","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"SNMP lets a monitoring system ask devices for structured information and receive notifications. It can report switch health without someone opening every device’s console.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP)\u003c/strong> connects a monitoring \u003cstrong>manager\u003c/strong> to an \u003cstrong>agent\u003c/strong>, the component on a managed device that answers requests. A manager might ask a switch whether interface 8 is up, or read its traffic counters. A printer might report its status through the same general system.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>SNMP defines management requests, values, and notifications. Even when it reports a Layer 1 cable problem or a Layer 2 switch interface, SNMP itself is an application protocol. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Queries to an agent\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Usually UDP 161\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Manager asks; agent returns a response.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Notifications to a receiver\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Usually UDP 162\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Traps and informs report an event.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read the device’s information\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>OID\u003c/strong>, or object identifier, is a structured numeric name for a managed object, such as a particular interface’s operating state. A \u003cstrong>MIB\u003c/strong>, or Management Information Base module, describes object names, types, and meanings. Think “field identifier” and “field dictionary,” not a second monitoring server.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>GET\u003c/strong> reads values; \u003cstrong>GETNEXT/GETBULK\u003c/strong> help retrieve successive objects; \u003cstrong>SET\u003c/strong> can change writable values when access permits. A \u003cstrong>trap\u003c/strong> is a notification without an SNMP acknowledgment. An \u003cstrong>inform\u003c/strong> requests acknowledgment, though delivery can still fail. Polls can miss a brief event between checks, so notifications and logs add useful context.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"snmp-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"snmp-lab-title\">Does “version 3” mean encrypted?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional switch returns the same interface-state value. Choose the security setting to inspect what changes. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-snmp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>SNMPv2c\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What protects the community string?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Manager supplies a community\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>A shared string controls the configured access.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Traffic is readable\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>SNMPv2c provides no message encryption.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Agent returns interface state\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>An on-path observer may read it too.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>A community string is not an encrypted tunnel. Read-only access can limit writes but does not hide the query or response.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the approved management network and restrict allowed managers. Plan supported, properly configured SNMPv3 access.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-snmp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>SNMPv3 · authNoPriv\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Authentication is on. Is the value hidden?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Manager authenticates messages\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The configured authentication checks protect integrity.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>No privacy enabled\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The managed values are not encrypted.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Agent returns interface state\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The payload can remain readable on the path.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Authentication verifies the message under the configured credentials; it does not by itself provide confidentiality.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the security level as well as the version. The name authNoPriv literally means authentication without privacy.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-snmp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>SNMPv3 · authPriv\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What does privacy add?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Authentication enabled\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Messages are checked under the configured credentials.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Privacy enabled\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The scoped management payload is encrypted.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Authorized manager reads it\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The receiver decrypts and interprets the value.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>authPriv adds confidentiality to authentication and integrity. Some message metadata remains visible.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use supported current algorithms and least-privilege views. Encryption does not stop an authorized account from making an allowed SET change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>What an analyst should check\u003c/h2>\u003cp>SNMPv3 also has \u003cstrong>noAuthNoPriv\u003c/strong>, with neither message authentication nor privacy. Never stop at “it is v3” when reviewing security. Check the actual level, permitted objects, read/write rights, and permitted source systems.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a fictional “switch stopped reporting” ticket, check the manager’s last response, the agent, routing/firewall path, and credentials. No response does not prove the switch is powered off. Compare \u003ca href=\"#note-syslog\">Syslog\u003c/a> events and other health evidence. SNMP usually supplies structured values and notifications; Syslog carries event messages. Neither one automatically explains why a device failed.\u003c/p>","question":"Why is SNMPv3 alone not enough to claim that management traffic is encrypted?","answer":"Its security level matters. noAuthNoPriv has neither authentication nor privacy; authNoPriv adds authentication and integrity; authPriv also encrypts the scoped management payload.","sources":["snmpOperations","snmpSecurity","snmpTransport","syslog"],"related":["syslog","switching","siem","firewalls","osi"],"keywords":"SNMP v1 v2c v3 161 162 UDP MIB OID GET SET trap inform authPriv authNoPriv monitoring","references":[{"title":"RFC 3416 — SNMP operations","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3416/"},{"title":"RFC 3414 — SNMPv3 user-based security","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3414/"},{"title":"RFC 3417 — SNMP transport mappings","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3417/"},{"title":"RFC 5424 — Syslog","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5424"}]},{"slug":"smtp","title":"SMTP / SMTPS","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"SMTP moves outgoing mail from a sender to mail servers and between servers. It does not read your inbox; IMAP and POP3 handle mailbox access.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP)\u003c/strong> carries outgoing messages. There are two useful jobs to separate: \u003cstrong>submission\u003c/strong>, when a client hands mail to its sending service, and \u003cstrong>relay\u003c/strong>, when one mail server passes it to another. Delivery to a mailbox and a person reading it are further steps.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>SMTP defines how applications identify senders and recipients, transfer a message, and report acceptance or rejection. Those mail-specific rules operate above TCP. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">SMTP relay\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 25\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Common server-to-server delivery; may use STARTTLS.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Message submission\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 587\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Common client submission with required STARTTLS.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Submission with implicit TLS\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 465\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often called SMTPS; TLS begins immediately.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Trace the message, one handoff at a time\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A client submits a message to its configured mail service. That service can use the recipient domain’s \u003ca href=\"#note-dns\">DNS\u003c/a> MX records to find receiving mail servers. The servers exchange SMTP replies as the message is accepted, queued, relayed, or rejected. The recipient later reads the mailbox through \u003ca href=\"#note-imap\">IMAP\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#note-pop3\">POP3\u003c/a>, or another supported access method.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>250 response after the message data\u003c/strong> means that server accepted responsibility for delivery or relay. It is not a read receipt or proof that the message reached the inbox. \u003cstrong>4xx\u003c/strong> replies indicate temporary failure; \u003cstrong>5xx\u003c/strong> indicate permanent failure for that attempt. Always read the full reply and the stage at which it occurred.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"smtp-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"smtp-lab-title\">“Sent” is only one checkpoint\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional employee emails a supplier. Predict what the evidence establishes at each step. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-smtp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Submission accepted\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Does 250 mean the supplier read it?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Laptop submits\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The sending service receives the complete message.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Server replies 250\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It accepts responsibility for the next delivery steps.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Recipient state unknown\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>No mailbox or read evidence is shown.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The submission succeeded. The example does not yet establish final delivery or reading.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the message identifier and timestamp to trace subsequent hops instead of asking the user to keep resending.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-smtp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Receiving server replies 451\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Is this a permanent rejection?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Sending server connects\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It attempts the next delivery handoff.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Temporary failure\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The receiver replies with a 4xx error.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Delivery is deferred\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The sending system can queue and retry.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>This attempt failed temporarily. The message has not been shown as delivered.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the full reason, queue status, and retry history. Report a delay without promising a delivery time unsupported by evidence.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-smtp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Receiving server replies 550\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Should the same request be retried unchanged?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Server checks recipient\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>This fictional reply says the mailbox does not exist.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Permanent failure\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The receiver rejects that delivery attempt.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Sender needs review\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The address or other stated cause must be checked.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>A 5xx reply calls for reviewing the stated cause. This example is an invalid recipient; other 550 replies can describe different policies.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Confirm the address through an established source and preserve the nondelivery report. Do not assume all 550 errors have one cause.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Encryption and email trust\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>SMTPS\u003c/strong> commonly means SMTP submission inside TLS from the start on 465. Port 587 commonly uses \u003cstrong>STARTTLS\u003c/strong>: begin SMTP, then upgrade before protected submission. For exam study, some objectives use “SMTPS” broadly for secure submission on 587; in real configuration, distinguish the port and TLS mode. Require the provider’s secure settings rather than guessing from the label.\u003c/p>\u003cp>SMTP TLS protects a particular connection, not necessarily every relay or the message after storage. \u003ca href=\"#note-email-auth\">SPF, DKIM, and DMARC\u003c/a> address domain authentication and handling policy; they do not encrypt the body or prove a payment request is genuine. During a mail incident, separate transport problems, mailbox access, and the legitimacy of the message.\u003c/p>","question":"Which protocols send mail and read mail, and what does an SMTP 250 after DATA prove?","answer":"SMTP submits and relays mail; IMAP and POP3 provide mailbox access. A 250 after the complete message data means that server accepted responsibility for delivery or relay, not that the recipient received or read it.","sources":["smtpProtocol","smtpSubmission","mailTLS","netObjectives","dmarc"],"related":["imap","pop3","email-auth","dns","bec","osi"],"keywords":"SMTP SMTPS 25 465 587 STARTTLS email submission relay MX 250 451 550 queued delivery","references":[{"title":"RFC 5321 — Simple Mail Transfer Protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc5321/"},{"title":"RFC 6409 — Message submission","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc6409/"},{"title":"RFC 8314: TLS for email submission and access","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8314"},{"title":"CompTIA Network+ N10-009: official exam objectives","url":"https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-network-n10-009-exam-objectives-(4-0)-(1).pdf"},{"title":"Microsoft — DMARC and alignment","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dmarc-configure"}]},{"slug":"rpc-netbios","title":"RPC / NetBIOS","category":"IT operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"RPC asks another computer to perform a software operation. NetBIOS provides older naming and communication services. They often appear in Windows troubleshooting, but they are different technologies.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Remote Procedure Call (RPC)\u003c/strong> lets a program ask a service on another machine to do work and return a result. A Windows management console might request information from a remote service this way. \u003cstrong>NetBIOS\u003c/strong> is an older interface and set of services for names, datagrams, and sessions; it is not another name for RPC.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Where they fit in OSI\u003c/h2>\u003cp>RPC’s remote operations are usually discussed at the application layer, \u003cstrong>Layer 7\u003c/strong>. Establishing and maintaining a logical conversation illustrates \u003cstrong>Layer 5\u003c/strong> session responsibilities. NetBIOS’s session service is a common Layer 5 study example. Its name and datagram services have different jobs. Explain the specific function rather than insisting that every part of either technology occupies one neat layer.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Windows RPC endpoint mapper\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 135\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Finds where a requested RPC service is listening.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">RPC over dynamic TCP\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Negotiated server port\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Modern Windows commonly uses 49152–65535; configuration can differ.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">NetBIOS name service\u003c/th>\u003ctd>UDP 137\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Legacy name registration and resolution.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">NetBIOS datagram service\u003c/th>\u003ctd>UDP 138\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Connectionless messages.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">NetBIOS session service\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 139\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Connection-oriented sessions; can carry legacy SMB.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Direct-hosted SMB\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 445\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Modern SMB commonly works without NetBIOS; can also carry RPC named pipes.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>An introduction is not the whole conversation\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The RPC \u003cstrong>endpoint mapper\u003c/strong> is like a reception desk. A client asks for a particular service and receives its endpoint. With dynamically assigned TCP endpoints, the client then makes another connection to that returned port. The mapper can be reachable while the actual service is blocked, stopped, or not registered.\u003c/p>\u003cp>RPC has several transports; the two-step TCP example is not the only arrangement. Some Windows RPC traffic uses \u003cstrong>named pipes\u003c/strong>, a software communication channel carried over \u003ca href=\"#note-smb\">SMB\u003c/a>. This is why “RPC uses 135” is a starting point, not a complete firewall design.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"rpc-netbios-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"rpc-netbios-lab-title\">Follow the second connection\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional administration console requests a service on SERVER-01. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-rpc-netbios\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Port 135 responds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Is the requested operation guaranteed to work?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client asks the mapper\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>TCP 135 is reachable.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Mapper returns 50010\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>This is the fictional service endpoint.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Second path is blocked\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The operation cannot reach TCP 50010.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The introduction succeeded while the actual service connection failed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the returned endpoint and scoped firewall path. Do not open all high ports to every system as a shortcut.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-rpc-netbios\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The service endpoint responds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Does this prove the user has permission?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Endpoint is reached\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The requested service receives the call.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Account is evaluated\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The service applies authentication and access checks.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Access is denied\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The fictional account lacks the required right.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>A listening service and successful network path do not grant the caller permission to perform every operation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Record the operation, account, service, and error. Check intended rights rather than weakening the firewall.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-rpc-netbios\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>SMB works with NetBIOS disabled\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Is that contradictory?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>DNS resolves FILES-01\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client obtains the server address.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client connects to 445\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Direct-hosted SMB carries file operations.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Share opens\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>No TCP 139 session was needed here.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Modern direct-hosted SMB does not inherently require NetBIOS. Name resolution can use DNS instead.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inventory legacy dependencies before changing NetBIOS settings. One working share does not prove every older application is independent of it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>A useful Windows troubleshooting order\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For “RPC server unavailable,” establish the target name and address, the requested service, whether it is running, and which transport it uses. Then check the mapper or named-pipe path as applicable, the returned endpoint, and access controls. Preserve the exact error and time.\u003c/p>\u003cp>You may meet these dependencies around remote management and \u003ca href=\"#note-windows-domain\">Active Directory\u003c/a>. Distinguishing name resolution, service discovery, transport, and authorization makes the error far more useful than assuming the whole server is offline.\u003c/p>","question":"Why does a successful TCP 135 test not prove a Windows RPC operation will succeed?","answer":"135 commonly reaches the endpoint mapper. The requested service may use another negotiated port or a different transport. That path, the service itself, and the caller’s rights must also work. NetBIOS is a separate family of naming and communication services.","sources":["rpcGuide","windowsPorts","netbiosProtocol","smb"],"related":["smb","windows-domain","dns","firewalls","osi"],"keywords":"RPC NetBIOS endpoint mapper 135 137 138 139 445 dynamic ports 49152 65535 named pipes Windows session layer","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Troubleshooting RPC errors","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-client/networking/rpc-errors-troubleshooting"},{"title":"Microsoft: Windows service overview and network port requirements","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/service-overview-and-network-port-requirements"},{"title":"RFC 1002 — NetBIOS services over TCP/UDP","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc1002/"},{"title":"Microsoft — SMB file sharing","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/file-server-smb-overview"}]},{"slug":"http","title":"HTTP / HTTPS","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"HTTP is the request-and-response language of the web. HTTPS carries HTTP through a protected connection, helping keep the exchanged content private and intact.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP)\u003c/strong> lets a client ask a server for a resource and receive a response. A browser is one client; an application calling an API is another. An \u003cstrong>API\u003c/strong> is an interface that software uses to request data or actions. Both can exchange HTTP messages.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>HTTP defines application requests, response status codes, headers, and content. HTTPS keeps that application purpose while adding TLS protection underneath it. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">HTTP\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Commonly TCP 80\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No built-in TLS protection.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">HTTPS over HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Commonly TCP 443\u003c/td>\u003ctd>HTTP inside TLS over TCP.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">HTTPS over HTTP/3\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Commonly UDP 443\u003c/td>\u003ctd>HTTP over QUIC, which incorporates TLS security.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Read a web conversation\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A request has a \u003cstrong>method\u003c/strong>, such as GET to retrieve a resource or POST to submit data, and a \u003cstrong>target\u003c/strong>, such as \u003ccode>/reports\u003c/code>. \u003cstrong>Headers\u003c/strong> carry metadata such as content type or authentication information. A \u003cstrong>body\u003c/strong> carries the actual submitted or returned content when present. POST does not itself encrypt that content.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The server returns a status and, often, content. \u003cstrong>200\u003c/strong> means success; \u003cstrong>301/302\u003c/strong> indicate redirects; \u003cstrong>401\u003c/strong> means valid authentication credentials are needed; \u003cstrong>403\u003c/strong> means the request is understood but refused; \u003cstrong>404\u003c/strong> means the resource was not found or is not being disclosed. \u003cstrong>500\u003c/strong> is a server error, \u003cstrong>502\u003c/strong> an invalid upstream response at a gateway/proxy, and \u003cstrong>503\u003c/strong> temporary service unavailability. The response body and logs provide the specific reason.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"http-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"http-lab-title\">Find the last working step\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional employee opens https://portal.example/reports. A proxy is a server that handles a request on behalf of another server. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-http\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The browser gets HTTP 403\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Is the network path completely broken?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Name and connection work\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The browser reaches an HTTPS endpoint.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>HTTP request arrives\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>An HTTP component evaluates /reports.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Response is 403\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>That component refuses the request.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>An HTTP response came back. Start with access or request policy at the responding component rather than treating this as total network failure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A proxy or web firewall can generate the response too. Identify its source and reason; 403 does not prove the origin application saw the request.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-http\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The certificate name is wrong\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Should the login page be trusted anyway?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Browser reaches an endpoint\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>A server presents a TLS certificate.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Hostname check fails\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The certificate does not match portal.example.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Identity is unverified\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The normal protected request should not proceed.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Reachability is not the same as verified server identity. The certificate problem comes before ordinary application troubleshooting.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the requested name, DNS, certificate, and any approved inspection proxy. Do not bypass the warning to enter credentials.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-http\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The proxy returns HTTP 502\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Which hop needs attention?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Browser reaches proxy\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client-side HTTPS path works.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Proxy contacts the app\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It receives an invalid upstream response in this case.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Browser sees 502\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The proxy reports the upstream failure.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>One connection can work while a later connection fails. A 502 points toward the proxy-to-upstream interaction.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Correlate the proxy log, application health, destination, and timestamp. Do not claim the employee’s whole internet connection is down.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>What HTTPS does and does not prove\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Properly validated \u003ca href=\"#note-tls\">TLS\u003c/a> protects traffic and checks the server identity against the requested name. It does not certify that a business is honest or a download is harmless. A phishing site can have a valid certificate for its own domain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a web incident or outage, collect the exact URL, time, status, responding component, and relevant request identifier. Remove credentials, session cookies, and tokens from shared evidence. Separate \u003ca href=\"#note-dns\">DNS\u003c/a>, connection establishment, TLS validation, and the HTTP response; each gives a different troubleshooting clue.\u003c/p>","question":"An HTTPS page returns 403. What does that tell you, and what does it not tell you?","answer":"You received an HTTP refusal from some responding component. Investigate its access or request policy and logs. It does not establish total network failure, prove the origin app received the request, or imply that HTTPS is broken.","sources":["httpSemantics","http3","tls","connectionTest"],"related":["tls","dns","tcp","phishing","osi"],"keywords":"HTTP HTTPS 80 443 TCP UDP QUIC HTTP3 GET POST status 200 301 302 401 403 404 500 502 503 TLS","references":[{"title":"RFC 9110 — HTTP semantics","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html"},{"title":"RFC 9114 — HTTP/3","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114"},{"title":"RFC 9325 — Secure use of TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9325.html"},{"title":"Microsoft — Test-NetConnection","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/test-netconnection"}]},{"slug":"ssh","title":"SSH","category":"IT operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"SSH provides protected remote access, commonly to a command line. It checks the server’s identity and then authenticates the user; those are two different checks.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Secure Shell (SSH)\u003c/strong> lets you run commands on another system over an encrypted connection. A technician might inspect a Linux server or manage a switch through SSH. The terminal on your laptop displays the remote system’s output; typing a command can change that remote system if your account has permission.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>SSH provides remote login, command execution, and other application services. Its own specification uses internal “transport,” “authentication,” and “connection” components; those names are not the OSI layer numbers. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">SSH\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Commonly TCP 22\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Remote command sessions and other SSH services.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">SFTP\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Commonly TCP 22\u003c/td>\u003ctd>File transfer through SSH, when the server provides it.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Telnet comparison\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 23\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ordinary Telnet does not encrypt the terminal session.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Two identities to understand\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>host key\u003c/strong> identifies the SSH server. Your client can remember its fingerprint, a short representation of the key, and compare it on later connections. A first-time prompt still needs verification against an approved trusted reference. Merely clicking “accept” does not independently prove the host is correct.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>User authentication\u003c/strong> comes next. It may use a password, a key, or another supported method. With public-key authentication, the server knows your public key and you prove possession of the private key without sending the private key to the server. Keep private keys protected. A passphrase can protect a stored private-key file; it is not the remote account password.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"ssh-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"ssh-lab-title\">Which identity check failed?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional technician connects to switch-01. Compare three outcomes before choosing the next investigation. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ssh\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The host key changed unexpectedly\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Is this just a bad user password?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client reaches the server\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>An SSH service answers.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Host key differs\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>It does not match the saved identity.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Verification needed\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client cannot assume this is the intended host.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>This is a server-identity warning. A rebuild, key rotation, wrong destination, or interception could explain it; the warning alone does not choose the cause.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Verify the current fingerprint through an approved independent source before updating the saved record. Do not blindly delete the old key to silence the warning.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ssh\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Host verified, user rejected\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What has already worked?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Host identity matches\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client establishes the protected connection.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>User authentication fails\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The offered account or key is not accepted.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>No command session\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The user has not gained access.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The network and host-identity checks worked in this example. The remaining failure is at user authentication.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the intended username, allowed method, key selection, and server authentication logs. Repeated guesses can create lockouts.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ssh\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Login works, a command is denied\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Did encryption stop working?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>SSH login succeeds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The server accepts the user.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Command needs more rights\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The remote operating system checks authorization.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Operation is denied\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The user lacks permission for this action.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Authentication identifies the user; authorization controls what that user can do. A denied command does not imply broken SSH encryption.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Confirm the intended role and approved task. Do not grant broad administrator rights just to make an unrelated permission error disappear.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>More than a terminal\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>SFTP\u003c/strong> transfers files through SSH and is different from \u003ca href=\"#note-ftp\">FTPS\u003c/a>, which adds TLS to FTP. SSH can also forward connections through a tunnel. That capability is useful for approved administration, but a tunnel should not become an undocumented path around access controls.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When reviewing remote access, record the source, destination, user, authentication result, and actual command evidence when available. A connection to TCP 22 does not by itself prove SSH, successful login, or malicious activity. Use \u003ca href=\"#note-alert-triage\">alert triage\u003c/a> to connect the network record with host and account evidence.\u003c/p>","question":"What is the difference between an SSH host key and a user’s authentication key?","answer":"The host key identifies the server you reached. A user authentication key helps prove which user is connecting. Verify the server identity, protect the user’s private key, and keep authentication separate from permission to run a command.","sources":["sshProtocol","sshManual","sftpManual","telnetProtocol"],"related":["telnet","ftp","rdp","alert-triage","osi"],"keywords":"SSH Secure Shell 22 host key fingerprint public key private key authentication SFTP tunnel remote command","references":[{"title":"RFC 4251 — Secure Shell architecture","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc4251/"},{"title":"OpenBSD — SSH client manual","url":"https://man.openbsd.org/ssh"},{"title":"OpenBSD — SFTP manual","url":"https://man.openbsd.org/sftp"},{"title":"RFC 854 — Telnet protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc854/"}]},{"slug":"rdp","title":"RDP","category":"IT operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"RDP lets you interact with a remote computer’s graphical desktop. The applications run on the remote host while your device sends input and receives the display.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)\u003c/strong> carries a remote desktop experience: screen updates, keyboard input, mouse actions, and supported redirected resources. For example, you might use an approved remote-access path to inspect a Windows server’s event logs. The event viewer runs on the server, even though you see its window on your laptop.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>RDP provides an application service for remote display and input. It also has session-related functions, but an ordinary “which layer?” classification places the remote-desktop protocol at the application layer. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Direct RDP\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Commonly TCP and UDP 3389\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TCP establishes the connection; supported UDP transport can improve the experience.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">RD Gateway\u003c/th>\u003ctd>HTTPS on TCP 443\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A gateway can provide the external path instead of exposing a host’s 3389 directly.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Reachable does not mean allowed\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A working session needs a reachable host, a listening service, compatible security settings, and an authorized account. \u003cstrong>Network Level Authentication (NLA)\u003c/strong> requires authentication before the full remote session is established. Despite the name, it is not a claim that RDP is an OSI Layer 3 protocol. It also does not mean multi-factor authentication is automatically enabled.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Remote access should follow the organization’s approved gateway, \u003ca href=\"#note-vpns\">VPN\u003c/a>, or \u003ca href=\"#note-ztna-sase\">ZTNA\u003c/a> design, with appropriate identity controls. Avoid treating direct internet exposure of 3389 as the routine way to provide support. Clipboard, drive, and printer redirection can move information across the session and should match the task’s policy.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"rdp-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"rdp-lab-title\">Where is the work running?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional technician uses a remote desktop on SERVER-01. Assume a normal Windows session without an immediate disconnect logoff policy. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-rdp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Open an application inside RDP\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Whose resources does it use?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Laptop sends input\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>A click requests an application launch.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>SERVER-01 runs the app\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The remote CPU, memory, and permissions apply.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Laptop receives the display\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client shows the remote application window.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The remote application is running on SERVER-01. A slow remote app is not automatically caused by the laptop’s CPU.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compare host performance with connection quality. Delay in input or screen updates can have a different cause from slow processing on the server.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-rdp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Close the RDP window\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Did this sign the user out?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client disconnects\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The visible connection ends.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Remote session remains\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Its applications can continue running.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Reconnection is possible\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The user may return to the existing session.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Disconnecting is different from signing out. Session time limits and policy can later end a disconnected session.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the intended sign-out procedure when work is complete, and check policy before assuming a task stopped merely because the window closed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-rdp\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>The port responds but sign-in fails\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Is the firewall necessarily the problem?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>TCP path succeeds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The RDP service can be reached.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Access check fails\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>This fictional account lacks the remote logon right.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>No desktop is granted\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The service denies the session.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The network check succeeded; authorization is the failure in this case.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the exact error, intended account, effective logon policy, and service logs. Do not disable NLA or broadly grant administrator access as a first response.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>What to record during a review\u003c/h2>\u003cp>For a failed connection, capture the exact target, time, approved access path, and whether the failure occurred before or after authentication. A gateway path has multiple hops, so its external port need not be the host’s RDP port.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For suspicious remote access, compare the source, user, session times, authorization, and observed activity. Legitimate support and attacker activity can both use RDP; the protocol name alone settles neither. \u003ca href=\"#note-windows-events\">Windows events\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#note-alert-triage\">alert triage\u003c/a> help distinguish a connection attempt from a successful and authorized session.\u003c/p>","question":"What is the difference between disconnecting an RDP window and signing out of Windows?","answer":"Disconnecting ends the client connection, while the remote session and programs may remain until policy ends them. Signing out ends that user session. The applications run on the remote host, not inside the local RDP client.","sources":["rdpPorts","rdpAccess","rdpTroubleshooting","windowsPorts"],"related":["ssh","vpns","ztna-sase","windows-events","osi"],"keywords":"RDP Remote Desktop 3389 TCP UDP gateway 443 NLA Network Level Authentication disconnect sign out session","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Ports used by Remote Desktop Services","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/remote/ports-used-by-rds"},{"title":"Microsoft — Remote Desktop access and NLA","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/remote/remote-desktop-services/remotepc/remote-desktop-allow-access"},{"title":"Microsoft — Remote Desktop connection troubleshooting","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/remote/rdp-error-general-troubleshooting"},{"title":"Microsoft: Windows service overview and network port requirements","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/service-overview-and-network-port-requirements"}]},{"slug":"smb","title":"SMB","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"SMB lets a client work with files and other shared resources on a server. A mapped drive is a convenient view of that service, not a copy of the entire server folder.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Server Message Block (SMB)\u003c/strong> is a network file-sharing protocol widely used by Windows and supported by other systems, including many NAS devices. A NAS is storage made available over a network. When you open \u003ccode>\\\\files-01\\Finance\u003c/code>, the client asks the server to perform operations on that share.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>SMB defines application operations such as opening a file, reading bytes, and accessing a share. Ethernet switching and TCP delivery carry the conversation but do not interpret file permissions. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Direct-hosted SMB\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Commonly TCP 445\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Modern Windows file-sharing path.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">SMB over NetBIOS sessions\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 139\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Legacy arrangement; not required for direct-hosted SMB.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>From connection to file access\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The client and server first negotiate a supported \u003cstrong>dialect\u003c/strong>, meaning an SMB protocol version. They establish a session using the configured authentication method, connect to a named share, and request file operations. \u003cstrong>Authentication\u003c/strong> establishes the account; \u003cstrong>authorization\u003c/strong> decides whether that account can perform the requested action.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A mapped drive letter such as Z: is a shortcut to a network path. It does not bypass server checks. On Windows, access over a share is constrained by both share permissions and filesystem permissions. A user might read a report yet be unable to save changes. The \u003ca href=\"#note-file-shares\">File shares\u003c/a> note covers that support workflow in more detail.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"smb-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"smb-lab-title\">Access, integrity, or confidentiality?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional employee opens a Finance share. Each case changes a different part of the SMB conversation. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-smb\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Login succeeds, file access is denied\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>What did the successful login prove?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Session identifies the user\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The server accepts the account.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client requests a file\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The share and file access rules are evaluated.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Read is denied\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>This account lacks the required permission.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>An authenticated session does not grant access to every share or file.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the exact path, user, group membership, and effective share/filesystem permissions. Changing a firewall rule will not grant a missing file right.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-smb\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Signing is enabled, encryption is off\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Can the file contents still be readable on the path?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client sends an SMB message\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The message is signed using session keys.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Integrity is checked\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Tampering can be detected by the receiver.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Content is not hidden\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Signing alone does not encrypt the file payload.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Signing protects message integrity and authenticity. It is different from confidentiality.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Verify the negotiated security of the actual session. Do not write “file traffic is encrypted” based only on a signing setting.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-smb\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>SMB encryption is negotiated\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Does it give the user more permissions?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Session supports encryption\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The endpoints negotiate a protected SMB exchange.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Network payload is encrypted\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Authorized endpoints can decrypt the SMB content.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>File rights still apply\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The server still decides whether access is allowed.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Encryption protects traffic; it does not expand the user’s access rights or encrypt every file at rest.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Confirm protection on the required share/session and keep access scoped to the employee’s actual job.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Security and evidence to remember\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>SMB1\u003c/strong> is an obsolete dialect with serious security limitations. Inventory old dependencies and use supported modern SMB configurations. \u003cstrong>Signing\u003c/strong> detects message alteration; \u003cstrong>encryption\u003c/strong> hides SMB content in transit. Available features and defaults depend on both endpoints and configuration, so verify what was actually negotiated.\u003c/p>\u003cp>File sharing is useful for normal work and can also be abused to reach other systems or data. Review source, account, destination share, action, and time. A TCP 445 connection does not prove that a file was read, written, or stolen. Modern deployments can also use SMB over QUIC with a different transport; TCP 445 remains the core direct-hosted association to learn first.\u003c/p>","question":"What does SMB signing protect, and how is it different from SMB encryption?","answer":"Signing protects integrity and authenticity of SMB messages but does not hide their contents. Encryption protects SMB content in transit. Neither replaces the server’s authentication and share/file authorization checks.","sources":["smb","smbSigning","smbSecurity","windowsPorts"],"related":["file-shares","rpc-netbios","windows-domain","malware","firewalls","osi"],"keywords":"SMB Server Message Block 445 139 file sharing NAS mapped drive UNC SMB1 SMB3 signing encryption permissions","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — SMB file sharing","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/file-server-smb-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — SMB signing","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-signing-overview"},{"title":"Microsoft — SMB security hardening","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/smb-security-hardening"},{"title":"Microsoft: Windows service overview and network port requirements","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/service-overview-and-network-port-requirements"}]},{"slug":"ldap","title":"LDAP / LDAPS","category":"Identity & access","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"LDAP lets applications query and update a directory of objects such as users, groups, and computers. LDAPS protects the directory conversation with TLS from the start.","body":"\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP)\u003c/strong> is a language for working with a directory. Think of a structured company address book: an entry represents an object, and its attributes describe it. A user entry might contain a display name and email address. Access rules determine which information an application can read or change.\u003c/p>\u003caside class=\"protocol-layer\">\u003cspan class=\"protocol-layer-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">7\u003c/span>\u003cdiv>\u003cstrong>OSI · Application layer\u003c/strong>\u003cp>LDAP defines directory operations such as binding, searching, and modifying entries. Those operations are application-level requests, even when the directory is used to support network sign-in. TCP or UDP carries its messages at Layer 4.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/aside>\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Common server ports\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ctable class=\"protocol-ports\">\u003ccaption>Common ports · server side\u003c/caption>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Service\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Transport / port\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What to remember\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">LDAP\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 389\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can upgrade to TLS using StartTLS.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">LDAPS\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 636\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TLS begins immediately, before LDAP operations.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">AD Global Catalog\u003c/th>\u003ctd>TCP 3268 / 3269 with TLS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Searches across an AD forest (a collection of related domains).\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"protocol-caption\">These are common defaults, not proof of the application or its security. A TCP/UDP port identifies a software endpoint; it is different from a physical switch port.\u003c/p>\u003ch2>Bind, search, and permissions\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>bind\u003c/strong> establishes an authentication state for an LDAP session. A \u003cstrong>search\u003c/strong> asks for entries matching a filter within a selected part of the directory. A \u003cstrong>distinguished name (DN)\u003c/strong> identifies an entry by its position, for example \u003ccode>CN=Alex,OU=People,DC=corp,DC=example\u003c/code>. Here CN names the entry, OU names its organizational unit, and DC components describe the directory domain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A successful bind does not mean the account can read or edit every object. The \u003cstrong>search base\u003c/strong> says where to start; the \u003cstrong>scope\u003c/strong> says how far below it to look; the \u003cstrong>filter\u003c/strong> says what to match. A correctly authenticated application can return no matches simply because it searched the wrong branch.\u003c/p>\u003csection class=\"protocol-lab\" aria-labelledby=\"ldap-lab-title\">\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-lab-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">TRY IT / FICTIONAL CASES\u003c/span>\u003ch2 id=\"ldap-lab-title\">Why can a directory lookup fail?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional application looks for Alex under the People organizational unit. Make a prediction, then open a case to follow the result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ldap\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">01\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>TLS succeeds, bind fails\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Has the application signed in?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Client reaches TCP 636\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The directory service answers.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>TLS is established\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The certificate is validated in this example.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Bind is rejected\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The supplied credentials are not accepted.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The protected connection works, but the directory has not accepted the requested authentication.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the bind identity, credential state, allowed method, and exact directory error. Do not remove TLS to solve an authentication failure.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ldap\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">02\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Bind succeeds, search returns no match\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Does this prove Alex does not exist?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Application binds\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Its account is accepted.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Search starts under Devices\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The fictional filter looks in the wrong branch.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>No match is returned\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Alex is actually stored under People.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>The query scope can explain the empty result. Zero matches does not prove absence from the entire directory.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Check the search base, scope, filter, and read rights before creating a duplicate user or changing permissions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"protocol-case\" name=\"protocol-ldap\">\u003csummary>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\">03\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003cstrong>Use port 389 with required StartTLS\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Must the directory data be plaintext?\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-case-toggle\" aria-hidden=\"true\">+\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"protocol-case-body\">\u003col class=\"protocol-path\" aria-label=\"Example sequence\">\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">01\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>LDAP connection starts\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client requests a TLS upgrade.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">02\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>TLS completes and is validated\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>The client requires success before continuing.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cspan class=\"protocol-step\">03\u003c/span>\u003cstrong>Protected LDAP operations\u003c/strong>\u003cspan>Bind and subsequent directory work use the encrypted channel.\u003c/span>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp class=\"protocol-outcome\">\u003cstrong>What this tells you\u003c/strong>Port 389 can carry TLS-protected LDAP after StartTLS. Port number alone is not evidence of plaintext credentials.\u003c/p>\u003cp>LDAPS on 636 starts TLS immediately. Match the client’s mode to the server and validate the certificate in either arrangement.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\u003ch2>Active Directory and secure configuration\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"#note-windows-domain\">Active Directory\u003c/a> uses LDAP for directory access, but AD is bigger than LDAP. DNS helps locate services, Kerberos commonly authenticates users, and other protocols support sharing, management, and replication. Seeing one LDAP connection does not describe the whole domain login process.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A simple bind that supplies a name and password needs a protected channel to keep that password confidential. Do not infer protection from the word “bind” or an open port. LDAPS and required StartTLS protect traffic; LDAP signing and channel-binding controls address related integrity and authentication concerns and are not interchangeable labels for encryption. For a support ticket, record the endpoint, TLS mode, bind result, and query details separately.\u003c/p>","question":"LDAP authentication succeeds but a search returns zero entries. What should you check before claiming the user is missing?","answer":"Check the search base, scope, filter, and the account’s read permissions. A successful bind only establishes authentication. It does not prove the query covered the right directory branch or can see every object.","sources":["ldapProtocol","ldapSecurity","ldapCertificates","windowsPorts","kerberos"],"related":["windows-domain","dns","rpc-netbios","tls","osi"],"keywords":"LDAP LDAPS 389 636 3268 3269 StartTLS bind search directory DN distinguished name Active Directory","references":[{"title":"RFC 4511 — LDAP operations","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc4511/"},{"title":"RFC 4513 — LDAP authentication and TLS","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc4513/"},{"title":"Microsoft — Certificates for LDAPS","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/configure-ldap-signing-certificates"},{"title":"Microsoft: Windows service overview and network port requirements","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/troubleshoot/windows-server/networking/service-overview-and-network-port-requirements"},{"title":"Microsoft — Kerberos authentication","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/kerberos/kerberos-authentication-overview"}]},{"slug":"alert-triage","title":"Alert triage","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Triage means deciding what an alert shows, how urgent it is, and who needs to act next. An alert starts a question; it does not finish an investigation.","body":"\u003ch2>Start with the difference between an event and an alert\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>event\u003c/strong> is something recorded: a sign-in, a process starting, or a blocked connection. An \u003cstrong>alert\u003c/strong> is a tool calling attention to one or more events. An \u003cstrong>incident\u003c/strong> is a security situation that needs coordinated investigation or response; several alerts may belong to it. Opening ten alerts does not necessarily mean ten separate attacks.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Triage\u003c/strong> is the first assessment. You gather enough context to decide urgency, assign an owner, and choose the next useful action. You may then investigate further, escalate, or close the alert with a supported explanation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Read an alert like a short case file\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Identify the scope:\u003c/strong> which customer, device, account, and time zone? Is this a user laptop or a server supporting everyone?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Read the trigger:\u003c/strong> which behavior matched the detection? A scary alert title is less useful than its underlying events.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Check the context:\u003c/strong> parent process, command, file location, sign-in details, related messages, and approved work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Read the action result:\u003c/strong> was something detected, blocked, quarantined, or isolated? Did the requested action actually succeed?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Choose and record the next step:\u003c/strong> what evidence is missing, who owns it, and when will they review it?\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"triage\" aria-label=\"One alert, three different stories\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>One alert, three different stories\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the evidence behind a suspicious script alert. Choose the conclusion the record supports.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>A script approved for a documented maintenance task may be benign. Active encryption requires urgent response. A disconnected sensor leaves an investigation gap; it does not prove safety.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Severity, confidence, and priority are different\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Severity\u003c/strong> describes potential seriousness. \u003cstrong>Confidence\u003c/strong> describes how strongly the evidence supports the detection. \u003cstrong>Priority\u003c/strong> combines those with current impact, asset importance, ongoing activity, and your response procedures. A medium-severity alert showing active harm on a business-critical system can need attention before a high-severity alert already verified as authorized testing.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A familiar administrator name is context, not proof of authorization. Confirm the exact task, device, time, and approval. A legitimate tool can be used maliciously. Conversely, a command that looks unusual may be part of normal maintenance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Classify only what you can support\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>true positive\u003c/strong> generally means the detection correctly identified the security activity of concern. A \u003cstrong>false positive\u003c/strong> means the detection incorrectly identified that condition. Some products separately label real but authorized activity a \u003cstrong>benign positive\u003c/strong>. Use the platform’s definitions and explain the reason in the ticket. “Unconfirmed” is an investigation state, not a synonym for false positive.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>A useful handoff\u003c/strong>\u003cp>“At 14:08 UTC, the endpoint recorded a document launching a script. The isolation request is pending because the sensor is offline. The response lead owns alternate containment; execution scope remains under review.” This tells the next analyst what happened and what has not been verified.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2>Where this helps at work\u003c/h2>\u003cp>In a managed service provider (MSP), an IT/security company supporting several customers, alerts arrive from different tools and environments. Confirm the customer before taking action. Read the MDR provider’s investigation and completed actions, then make the customer-side tasks explicit. Avoid duplicate containment requests or closing a ticket just because another team touched it.\u003c/p>","question":"An EDR alert says “isolation requested,” but the device is offline. What can you report?","answer":"Report that isolation was requested and remains unverified. Record the last sensor contact, escalate alternate containment through the response owner, and keep scope open. A requested action is not a completed action.","sources":["triageGuide","incidentGuide"],"related":["mdr","siem","incident-response","windows-events"],"keywords":"SOC escalation queue severity priority false positive benign positive","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — Investigate security alerts","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/investigate-alerts"},{"title":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3 — Incident response","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final"}]},{"slug":"incident-response","title":"Incident response","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Incident response is the coordinated work of understanding a security incident, limiting harm, restoring trustworthy service, and learning from what happened.","body":"\u003ch2>What makes response different from an alert review?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An alert review may answer one question. An incident can involve devices, accounts, email, business operations, and several teams at once. \u003cstrong>Incident response\u003c/strong> gives that work an owner and a plan. The objective is to reduce harm while establishing what happened and restoring a service people can trust.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Preparation belongs here too: contact lists, available logs, tested backups, response authority, and practiced procedures determine what you can do when a real incident arrives. NIST’s current incident-response guidance integrates response with wider cybersecurity risk management. The stages below are a practical learning sequence, not a rule that every incident moves through neatly once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Learn the words that describe action state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Activity\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">What it means\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Fictional laptop case\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Investigation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Establish facts, scope, and remaining unknowns.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Review the process timeline and related sign-ins.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Containment\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Limit further harm or attacker access.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Verify that network isolation took effect.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Eradication\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Remove the cause and remaining attacker footholds.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rebuild if needed; remove persistence and address exposed credentials.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Recovery\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Restore service and validate it.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Test the rebuilt laptop and monitor after controlled reconnection.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Closure and improvement\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Confirm agreed criteria and record lessons.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Document scope, action results, business validation, and follow-up owners.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Persistence\u003c/strong> is a way an attacker keeps access, such as an unauthorized startup task. Deleting the first suspicious file does not establish that no other foothold exists. Likewise, containment reduces immediate harm but may leave the affected system unusable.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"incident\" aria-label=\"Can this incident be closed?\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Can this incident be closed?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Move through the evidence snapshots from one fictional laptop incident. Decide whether to contain, continue cleanup, validate recovery, or consider closure.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Isolation alone is containment. Closure requires the agreed investigation, remediation, recovery, and documentation checks, not merely a quiet alert queue.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Build a timeline and keep authority clear\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Record timestamps with their time zone, affected assets, source records, action owners, and actual results. Keep \u003cstrong>observations\u003c/strong> separate from \u003cstrong>interpretations\u003c/strong>: “one account sent 40 messages” is an observation; “the attacker stole every mailbox” needs different evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use the response plan’s authority for disruptive action. During active harm, authorized containment and evidence preservation may happen in parallel. Do not let routine paperwork delay an urgent authorized response. Also do not erase a device reflexively: you may destroy information needed to understand the incident. The response lead coordinates those tradeoffs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Communicate the current state\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A useful update names confirmed impact, completed actions, unresolved questions, the next owner, and a realistic next update time. “No additional affected devices found in the reviewed logs” is narrower and more defensible than “nothing else was affected.” Specify which records and time window you reviewed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>At an MSP\u003c/strong>\u003cp>An MDR team may contain a laptop while the customer’s IT team resets access and restores service. Track that handoff. Somebody must verify each action and decide whether the customer’s recovery requirements are satisfied.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2>After service returns\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Confirm the original weakness has been addressed, check that monitoring works, and obtain the relevant service owner’s validation. Capture lessons as concrete work: a missing log source to enable, a recovery procedure to test, or a control to improve. Follow-up work can have separate tickets and owners; record what remains instead of calling it completed.\u003c/p>","question":"Why does “the device is isolated” not mean “the incident is resolved”?","answer":"Isolation limits a network path. It does not establish the original cause, remove persistence, secure affected accounts, restore service, or validate recovery. Those tasks need their own evidence and owners.","sources":["incidentGuide","ransomware"],"related":["alert-triage","malware","bec"],"keywords":"containment eradication recovery evidence timeline handoff incident response IR","references":[{"title":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3 — Incident response","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final"},{"title":"CISA — StopRansomware Guide","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/stopransomware/ransomware-guide"}]},{"slug":"phishing","title":"Phishing","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Phishing uses a deceptive message to persuade someone to reveal information or take an unsafe action. The important question is what the message is trying to make the recipient do.","body":"\u003ch2>Look beyond spelling mistakes\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A phishing message might ask for a password, an MFA code, a payment, an attachment download, or approval for an application to access an account. It can arrive through email, text, collaboration chat, a QR code, or a phone conversation. Polished writing and a familiar logo do not make it trustworthy.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Spear phishing\u003c/strong> targets a particular person or organization. \u003cstrong>Credential harvesting\u003c/strong> uses a fake sign-in page to collect authentication information. \u003cstrong>Social engineering\u003c/strong> is the broader act of manipulating people; phishing is one delivery method. Some attacks use a compromised real account, so the address can look completely familiar.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Inspect the request, identity, and destination\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Request:\u003c/strong> is the sender asking for an unusual action, secrecy, urgent payment, or bypassing a normal process?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Identity:\u003c/strong> compare the display name, full sender address, and reply-to address with a trusted record.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Destination:\u003c/strong> inspect the actual link through approved analysis tools. The displayed text may differ from its target.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Context:\u003c/strong> verify the business request using a known contact channel, not the phone number included in the suspicious message.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>In the fictional address \u003ccode>https://login.example.com.verify-access.example.net/\u003c/code>, the hostname ends in \u003ccode>example.net\u003c/code>. Having \u003ccode>example.com\u003c/code> earlier in the name does not make it part of that organization’s domain. Domain boundaries vary, so use approved tools and trusted records for unfamiliar names. HTTPS protects a connection; it does not prove an honest sender or page.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"phishing\" aria-label=\"Read the request before trusting the badge\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Read the request before trusting the badge\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Compare three fictional messages. Decide which next action fits each message and the recipient’s actions so far.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>Passing email authentication does not validate a payment request. Verify unusual banking changes independently; escalate a reported credential submission; report unexpected credential links through the approved channel.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Work a reported-phishing ticket\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Thank the user and preserve the original report and message using the approved reporting method.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Capture message identifiers, sender and recipients, time zone, attachment names, and analysis findings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Establish what the user did: received, clicked, downloaded, executed, entered credentials, or approved an MFA prompt. These are different exposure states.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use mail and security records to check delivery, related recipients, and available click or execution evidence. Missing telemetry does not prove that nobody interacted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Coordinate the appropriate response: mail removal, account investigation, or endpoint investigation under the applicable procedure.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Authentication is one part of the evidence\u003c/h2>\u003cp>SPF, DKIM, and DMARC help assess domain authentication. They do not assess whether the sender’s request is honest. An attacker-controlled lookalike domain or compromised mailbox can send authenticated mail. Use the separate \u003ca href=\"#note-email-auth\">Email authentication\u003c/a> note for those mechanisms and \u003ca href=\"#note-bec\">Business email compromise\u003c/a> for payment and mailbox investigations.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>A practical first response\u003c/strong>\u003cp>“Thanks for reporting this. Please leave the message available for investigation and use the reporting process. Tell us whether you opened the link, entered information, or approved a prompt.” Calm, specific questions improve the evidence; blaming the user discourages future reports.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A public file-analysis service may share submitted samples. Use the organization’s approved analysis environment for customer messages and attachments. The goal is to investigate the report without exposing its contents or interacting with the attack on your everyday workstation.\u003c/p>","question":"A message passes DMARC and asks Finance to change a supplier’s bank details. Is it safe to approve?","answer":"No. DMARC concerns authenticated domain alignment, not the truth of the payment instruction. Verify the change through a trusted, independent supplier contact and the established payment process.","sources":["phishingGuide","dmarc","bec"],"related":["email-auth","bec","security-awareness","incident-response"],"keywords":"social engineering spear phishing credential harvesting QR smishing vishing reported email","references":[{"title":"CISA — Recognize and report phishing","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/2024-09/Secure-Our-World-Phishing-Tip-Sheet.pdf"},{"title":"Microsoft — DMARC and alignment","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/email-authentication-dmarc-configure"},{"title":"FBI — Business email compromise","url":"https://www.fbi.gov/how-we-can-help-you/common-frauds-and-scams/business-email-compromise"}]},{"slug":"malware","title":"Malware","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Malware is software used to cause harm or gain unauthorized access. Investigating it means understanding what ran and what changed, not just recognizing a threatening filename.","body":"\u003ch2>What malware can do\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Malicious software\u003c/strong> can steal information, disrupt a device, give someone remote control, or help another attack stage begin. A suspicious download is not automatically an infection: delivery, execution, and successful harm are separate events. Your evidence should show which of them occurred.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Term\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Plain meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trojan\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Software presented as something useful that also carries malicious behavior.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Worm\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Malware able to spread between systems without someone manually copying each infection.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ransomware\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Malware that blocks access, commonly by encrypting files. Some extortion attacks also steal data.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Infostealer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Malware aimed at information such as stored credentials, cookies, or documents.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Loader or downloader\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A component that introduces another malicious component.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>These categories can overlap. Legitimate administrative tools can also be used during an intrusion. Seeing PowerShell, a remote-management application, or a script is not sufficient by itself to label the activity malicious.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Reconstruct a process story\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>process\u003c/strong> is a running instance of a program. A \u003cstrong>parent process\u003c/strong> started another process, called its child. A \u003cstrong>process tree\u003c/strong> shows that relationship. In a fictional case, a document viewer starts a command interpreter, which downloads an executable and creates a startup task. That sequence gives you more to investigate than the executable name alone.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Collect the relevant account, device, timestamps, command lines, file path, cryptographic hash, network destinations, and action results. A \u003cstrong>hash\u003c/strong> is a fingerprint of file contents that helps compare copies. Matching a known malicious hash can be useful; a new hash does not prove a file is safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Read the protection result carefully\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Blocked:\u003c/strong> an attempted action was prevented; verify what was blocked and when.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Quarantined:\u003c/strong> an object was placed under a security tool’s control; earlier execution may still need review.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Process terminated:\u003c/strong> that running process was stopped; another foothold may remain.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Device isolated:\u003c/strong> network communication is restricted according to the product’s behavior; this is containment.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Antivirus and endpoint detection tools help detect or stop behavior. The \u003ca href=\"#note-mdr\">EDR, MDR, and XDR\u003c/a> note explains how endpoint signals and analysts work together. Here, the focus is the activity you need to understand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A fictional infection ticket\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An employee opened an attachment at 09:12 UTC. At 09:14, the EDR recorded a suspicious script and confirmed device isolation. Record those facts. Review the earlier activity, whether additional payloads ran, persistence, affected accounts, and other devices with related evidence. “Isolated” supports a containment update; it does not support “no data was stolen.”\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>What to do next\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Follow the response plan and owner’s direction for containment, evidence collection, cleanup or rebuilding, and account protection. Avoid casually rebooting or deleting evidence. If active harm is spreading, the response lead must balance immediate containment with preservation rather than waiting for perfect certainty.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2>Recovery should address the entry point\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Validate the rebuilt or remediated system, close the original weakness, restore needed data from verified sources, and monitor for recurrence. A clean scan is one observation, not universal proof of recovery. Document the scope examined and the checks completed so another analyst can explain why restoration was allowed.\u003c/p>","question":"Why should you review the process timeline after an antivirus tool quarantines a file?","answer":"Quarantine describes the file’s current handling. The timeline may show that it executed earlier, launched other processes, established persistence, or accessed credentials. Those possibilities require separate investigation.","sources":["ransomware","behavior","incidentGuide"],"related":["mdr","alert-triage","incident-response","vulnerability-management"],"keywords":"AV antivirus infection ransomware process tree persistence hash infostealer","references":[{"title":"CISA — StopRansomware Guide","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/stopransomware/ransomware-guide"},{"title":"SentinelOne — Behavioral detection and Storyline","url":"https://www.sentinelone.com/blog/decrypting-sentinelone-detection-the-behavioral-ai-engine-in-real-time-cwpp/"},{"title":"NIST SP 800-61 Rev. 3 — Incident response","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final"}]},{"slug":"vulnerability-management","title":"Vulnerability management","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Vulnerability management is the repeated work of finding weaknesses, deciding which matter most, fixing or reducing them, and verifying the result.","body":"\u003ch2>A finding is the start of the work\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>vulnerability\u003c/strong> is a weakness that could be used to cause harm. Examples include an unpatched service, a weak configuration, or excessive access. A \u003cstrong>vulnerability assessment\u003c/strong> identifies and evaluates weaknesses. \u003cstrong>Vulnerability management\u003c/strong> is the ongoing process that turns findings into owned, verified improvements.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A scanner can help discover software versions, exposed services, and configuration issues. Its report is evidence to evaluate, not a complete description of business risk. A penetration test goes further by attempting authorized exploitation within agreed rules; running a vulnerability scan does not make the activity a penetration test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Configure an assessment with a purpose\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Define scope:\u003c/strong> confirm the customer, asset list, network ranges, exclusions, and maintenance constraints.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Choose the viewpoint:\u003c/strong> an external scan shows an internet-facing view; an internal scan shows what is reachable from its network position.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Choose depth:\u003c/strong> an authenticated scan uses authorized credentials to inspect local details. An unauthenticated scan has a more limited external view.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Confirm coverage:\u003c/strong> record unreachable assets, failed authentication, and skipped checks. Missing results are not the same as clean results.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Validate findings:\u003c/strong> check the detected version, affected configuration, vendor guidance, and existing mitigations before planning work.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2>Understand the labels\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>CVE\u003c/strong> is an identifier for a publicly disclosed vulnerability. It is not a severity score. \u003cstrong>CVSS\u003c/strong> describes vulnerability severity with a score and vector, the details used to calculate it. Record the version and score type; a base score alone is not a complete business-priority calculation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>CISA’s \u003cstrong>Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV)\u003c/strong> catalog identifies vulnerabilities with evidence of exploitation in the wild. KEV inclusion is an urgency signal, not proof that your particular device has been compromised. Absence from the catalog is not proof of safety. Combine threat evidence with exposure, prerequisites, asset importance, and current controls.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"vulnerability\" aria-label=\"Which finding gets attention first?\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Which finding gets attention first?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Compare three fictional findings, then change the environment. Choose a first priority and read the reasoning.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>An exposed, affected gateway with known exploitation may outrank a higher base score on a contained test system. If that test system becomes a critical exposed service, reassess the priority. Example scores are invented and do not refer to real CVEs.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Close the loop after a fix\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Assign an owner and target date. Plan patching through change management, including service testing and recovery options. If a patch cannot be applied immediately, document a specific mitigation, its evidence, and an expiry or review date. A mitigation reduces exposure; it may not remove the vulnerable software.\u003c/p>\u003cp>After the change, verify the running version and required restart, rescan with comparable coverage, and test the application. If the scanner loses access, do not interpret the missing finding as remediation. A suspected exploited weakness also needs an incident-response review; patching alone does not remove an attacker already present.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>A useful finding record\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Asset and owner → evidence and affected version → exposure and likely impact → priority rationale → fix or mitigation → verification result. This is the bridge between a scan report and completed security work.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","question":"A scanner reports no findings on a server, but authentication failed. What should you conclude?","answer":"The assessment has a coverage gap. Confirm which checks ran, repair authorized scan access, and repeat the relevant assessment. You cannot treat an unauthenticated or unreachable result as proof that the server is patched.","sources":["kev","cvss","changeGuide"],"related":["risk","security-controls","firewalls","malware"],"keywords":"vulnerability assessment scanning CVE CVSS KEV patching exposure remediation authenticated scan","references":[{"title":"CISA — Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog"},{"title":"FIRST — CVSS v4.0 User Guide","url":"https://www.first.org/cvss/v4.0/user-guide"},{"title":"NIST SP 800-128 — Security-focused configuration management","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/128/upd1/final"}]},{"slug":"risk","title":"Risk","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Risk describes how a possible event could harm something important and how likely that harm is. It helps a team decide where to spend attention and effort.","body":"\u003ch2>Turn a vague worry into a clear statement\u003c/h2>\u003cp>“Our network is insecure” is difficult to act on. A more useful statement is: “If a stolen support account can remotely administer the billing server without an additional check, an intruder could interrupt invoicing.” It names the route to harm and the business consequence.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A risk assessment does not promise to predict the future precisely. It makes assumptions, evidence, and uncertainty visible so an accountable owner can choose a response. A beginner’s job is often to help collect those facts rather than invent a numerical probability.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Term\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Meaning\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Fictional example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Asset\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Something of value.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Billing service and customer records.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Threat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A potential source or event that could cause harm.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Someone using stolen credentials.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Vulnerability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A weakness that the event could exploit.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Remote administration without the required additional verification.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Likelihood\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How plausible the harmful event is in this environment.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Consider exposure, observed attacks, and controls.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Impact\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The consequence if it happens.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Delayed invoices, service outage, or exposed records.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Control\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A safeguard that changes the risk.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Strong authentication and restricted administration paths.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2>Consider more than confidentiality\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Confidentiality\u003c/strong> means information stays available only to authorized parties. \u003cstrong>Integrity\u003c/strong> means it stays accurate and protected from unauthorized alteration. \u003cstrong>Availability\u003c/strong> means the service is usable when needed. These are often called the CIA triad. A ransomware outage is an availability problem even before you establish whether information was stolen.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Business impact depends on the asset. A short outage on a disposable practice machine differs from the same outage on the only scheduling system at a busy office. Ask the service owner about consequences, dependencies, and acceptable downtime.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>What can an organization do about a risk?\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Reduce it:\u003c/strong> implement safeguards or change the process.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Avoid it:\u003c/strong> stop the activity or remove the risky exposure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Share or transfer part of it:\u003c/strong> use a contractual arrangement or insurance. Operational responsibility does not simply disappear.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Accept it:\u003c/strong> an authorized risk owner records why the remaining risk is tolerable, for how long, and under which conditions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Inherent risk\u003c/strong> considers the scenario before relevant controls. \u003cstrong>Residual risk\u003c/strong> is what remains after controls. A control does not make risk automatically zero. A failed control or a changed environment should trigger another look.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Work through the decision\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A fictional company has one billing server and no verified restore test. Backups show “successful,” but their recoverability is unknown. Identify the asset and outage consequence, then propose a controlled restore test to a separate environment. This turns an assumption into evidence without overwriting the working service.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>What would a useful risk record contain?\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Service owner; scenario and business impact; current controls; evidence and gaps; likelihood and impact using the organization’s scale; proposed treatment; accountable decision owner; target and review dates. A successful backup job is evidence of a job result. A successful restore test supplies different evidence about recovery.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\n\u003ch2>Use scores as a conversation aid\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A high/medium/low matrix can organize priorities, but ratings depend on defined criteria. Do not mistake a vulnerability’s CVSS base score for the organization’s complete risk rating. Explain why a finding matters here and what observation would change your assessment.\u003c/p>","question":"How are a vulnerability and a risk different?","answer":"A vulnerability is a weakness. A risk describes possible harm involving an asset, a threat or event, likelihood, impact, and relevant controls. The same weakness can create different risk in different environments.","sources":["riskGuide","controlTests"],"related":["security-controls","vulnerability-management","incident-response"],"keywords":"CIA triad confidentiality integrity availability likelihood impact residual inherent risk assessment","references":[{"title":"NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 — Risk assessments","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/30/r1/final"},{"title":"NIST SP 800-53A Rev. 5 — Assessing controls","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/a/r5/final"}]},{"slug":"security-controls","title":"Security controls","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A security control is a safeguard intended to reduce risk. To know whether it works, you need a clear objective and evidence from the systems and people it covers.","body":"\u003ch2>A product is not the same as a working control\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Buying a firewall does not establish that guest devices are separated from a file server. The control is the implemented restriction: the intended traffic is denied, required traffic still works, and changes are managed. Tools help deliver a control; configuration, coverage, people, and verification determine whether it works.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Start with a requirement that can be tested. “Have good access security” is vague. “Standard staff accounts cannot administer the billing server” gives you a population, a prohibited action, and an expected result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Describe what a control does\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Function\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Purpose\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Preventive\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reduce the chance of an unwanted action.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A rule blocks guest access to a server network.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Detective\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reveal activity or a failure.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An alert identifies unexpected privileged sign-ins.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Corrective or recovery\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Limit consequences or restore an acceptable state.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An account-response procedure or tested data restoration.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>You can also describe how a control is delivered: \u003cstrong>technical\u003c/strong> measures such as access policies, \u003cstrong>administrative\u003c/strong> measures such as approvals and training, and \u003cstrong>physical\u003c/strong> measures such as controlled server-room entry. These classifications overlap. One safeguard can serve more than one purpose.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Design the test before declaring success\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Objective:\u003c/strong> what should be allowed or prevented, and for whom?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Coverage:\u003c/strong> which users, devices, applications, or locations should be included? Check exclusions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Implementation:\u003c/strong> examine the active configuration, not just a design document.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Behavior:\u003c/strong> test an authorized case that should succeed and an unauthorized case that should fail, within approved test scope.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Evidence:\u003c/strong> save the result, time, environment, and limitations. Assign an owner to any gap.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>NIST assessment guidance uses examination, interviews, and tests as complementary methods. A policy document shows intended behavior. An administrator can explain operation. A controlled test can show observed behavior. One does not always substitute for the others.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A fictional guest-network control\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The goal is: guests can use the internet but cannot initiate connections to the file server. A VLAN alone establishes a separate broadcast domain. Check routing and enforcement too. From a permitted test guest device, test the agreed blocked service and a permitted destination. Review the corresponding rule or log evidence.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>The rule exists, but a guest test succeeds. What does that mean?\u003c/summary>\u003cp>There is a control gap to investigate. Possible explanations include wrong rule order, the wrong source network, another routed path, or a host on the wrong VLAN. Record the actual test and address the cause. Do not declare the entire network exposed solely from one result, or ignore that result because the policy document says access is blocked.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\n\u003ch2>Keep controls working over time\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Least privilege\u003c/strong> grants only the access needed. \u003cstrong>Defense in depth\u003c/strong> uses complementary safeguards so one failure is less likely to cause complete loss. Neither means adding random restrictions: each layer should have a purpose and an owner.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Review configuration drift, exceptions, failed agents, and new assets. A compensating control is an alternative measure intended to address a requirement when the normal measure cannot be used; document its justification and test its effectiveness. Mark time-limited exceptions for review rather than letting them quietly become permanent.\u003c/p>","question":"Why test a permitted connection as well as a blocked one after a firewall change?","answer":"A control must enforce the intended restriction while preserving required service. A deny-all mistake could pass only the negative test. Together, permitted and denied cases help verify the actual objective.","sources":["controlTests","riskGuide","firewallGuide"],"related":["risk","firewalls","vlans","identity","security-awareness"],"keywords":"preventive detective corrective technical administrative physical least privilege defense in depth control gaps testing","references":[{"title":"NIST SP 800-53A Rev. 5 — Assessing controls","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/53/a/r5/final"},{"title":"NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 — Risk assessments","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/30/r1/final"},{"title":"NIST SP 800-41 Rev. 1 — Firewalls and firewall policy","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/41/r1/final"}]},{"slug":"security-awareness","title":"Security awareness","category":"Security operations","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Security awareness helps people recognize risky situations and know what to do next. Its value is safer behavior and faster reporting, not just completed training slides.","body":"\u003ch2>Make the useful action obvious\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An employee may not need to explain a phishing campaign’s infrastructure. They do need to know how to report a suspicious request, independently verify a payment change, and respond if they entered credentials. \u003cstrong>Awareness\u003c/strong> makes a risk recognizable. \u003cstrong>Training\u003c/strong> teaches a task. A continuing learning program supports both and checks whether the guidance works.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Different roles face different situations. Finance needs payment-verification practice. Administrators need to recognize unusual access requests and protect privileged accounts. Everyone benefits from knowing the reporting route and being able to use it without fear of embarrassment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Build a small learning cycle\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Choose a real need:\u003c/strong> for example, users do not know where to report a suspicious email.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Teach one behavior:\u003c/strong> demonstrate the approved reporting button or channel with a safe example.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Practice:\u003c/strong> use an authorized simulation or discussion that fits the audience.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Measure:\u003c/strong> check whether reports reach the right team and contain enough context.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Improve:\u003c/strong> remove confusing steps and repeat when roles or threats change.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>A platform such as KnowBe4 can help deliver training and simulations. The learning objective, scope, and follow-up process remain the organization’s responsibility. Tool navigation belongs in a separate tool guide; this note is about designing useful security habits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Use simulations to learn\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A simulated phishing message tests behavior under a designed set of conditions. Obtain the appropriate campaign approval, use an agreed audience, and keep the landing page and reporting process safe. Do not collect real passwords. A campaign should also test the support process: if someone reports it, does the team know what to do?\u003c/p>\u003cp>A click rate is not a complete measure of awareness. Message difficulty, audience, email security tools, and delivery affect results. Training completion measures participation, not mastery. Compare equivalent exercises and pair the figures with reporting quality, response time, and observed process improvements.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A fictional first-week exercise\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A new employee receives a message saying “Approve this unexpected MFA prompt so IT can update your laptop.” Ask them to choose a next step: approve it, ask the sender for reassurance, or deny the prompt and contact the known support channel.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Work through the safer response\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Deny the unexpected prompt and use the established support channel to report it. The sender who initiated the suspicious request is not an independent verifier. If the employee already approved it, respond calmly and start the relevant account review. That information is valuable evidence, not a reason to shame the reporter.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\n\u003ch2>Turn incidents into useful teaching\u003c/h2>\u003cp>After a real report, explain the relevant clue and the expected action using anonymized examples where appropriate. Avoid exposing customer details or naming individual employees in general training material. A short lesson about a specific decision is easier to remember than a long list of theoretical dangers.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>An analyst’s contribution\u003c/strong>\u003cp>Keep the reporting instructions accurate, investigate reports consistently, and tell users what happens next. A prompt, respectful response makes it more likely that the next person will report a problem early.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","question":"Why is a lower simulated-phishing click rate not enough to prove a training program works?","answer":"The result depends on the message, audience, delivery, and security tooling. Also check reporting behavior, response quality, practical decision-making, and whether the organization fixes the problems the exercise reveals.","sources":["learningGuide","phishingGuide"],"related":["phishing","security-controls"],"keywords":"KnowBe4 awareness training phishing simulations culture reporting","references":[{"title":"NIST SP 800-50 Rev. 1 — Security learning programs","url":"https://www.nist.gov/publications/building-cybersecurity-and-privacy-learning-program"},{"title":"CISA — Recognize and report phishing","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/2024-09/Secure-Our-World-Phishing-Tip-Sheet.pdf"}]},{"slug":"firewalls","title":"Firewalls","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A firewall decides which network traffic is permitted across a boundary. A useful rule describes who can reach what service, in which direction, and why.","body":"\u003ch2>Turn “open the port” into a specific request\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An application needs a client to reach a service, not every device to reach everything. Start with the \u003cstrong>source\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>destination\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>protocol\u003c/strong>, and \u003cstrong>destination port\u003c/strong>. A port is a numbered service endpoint in TCP or UDP. TCP 443 commonly carries HTTPS; TCP 445 commonly carries SMB file sharing. A port number is a clue, not proof of the application or its safety.\u003c/p>\u003cp>In a fictional office, staff on \u003ccode>10.20.10.0/24\u003c/code> need SMB access to one file server, \u003ccode>10.20.30.10\u003c/code>. Guests on \u003ccode>10.20.20.0/24\u003c/code> do not. That is a specific access requirement you can implement and test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Read a rule from left to right\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Field\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Example\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Source\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.20.10.0/24\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The staff subnet initiating the connection.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Destination\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10.20.30.10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The file server being contacted.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Protocol and destination port\u003c/td>\u003ctd>TCP 445\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The requested SMB service.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Action\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Allow\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Permit matching traffic at this enforcement point.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Placement and logging\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Before the general deny; log as required\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Order and visibility affect the result.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Many rule lists use \u003cstrong>first matching rule\u003c/strong> behavior: the first matching entry determines the result. A broad deny placed first can hide a later allow. A broad allow placed first can bypass a later restriction. Products differ in evaluation stages, policy types, and defaults; inspect the actual platform’s effective policy.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"firewall\" aria-label=\"Follow a connection through the rules\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Follow a connection through the rules\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Choose staff or guest traffic and a service. Move the specific staff rule above or below the broad deny, then inspect the first match.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>In this fictional first-match policy, staff SMB is allowed only when its specific allow precedes the broad deny. Guest traffic and other service requests are denied by this rule list.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Stateful does not mean “everything is allowed back”\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>stateful firewall\u003c/strong> tracks connections or flows so it can recognize return traffic associated with permitted communication. You normally do not need a broad rule allowing any new inbound connection just to receive replies. The details depend on the protocol and platform.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A network firewall controls traffic at a network boundary. A \u003cstrong>host firewall\u003c/strong> runs on the endpoint. A permitted network path can still fail at the host firewall or service. Even a successful TCP connection does not grant application or file permissions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Trace the path before changing policy\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>Confirm the client’s address, the resolved destination, protocol, and port.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check whether routing actually sends this flow through the firewall you are examining.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Review matching-rule information, action, and timestamps in available logs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check the destination listener and host firewall if the network boundary permits the flow.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test the intended permitted case and a case that must remain denied after an approved change.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>NAT\u003c/strong> changes addresses or ports; filtering decides permission. A firewall may perform both, but they are separate jobs. Address translation can change which addresses a rule or log shows. Check the platform’s evaluation order before comparing the original client address with a translated address.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"field-note\">\u003cstrong>At work\u003c/strong>\u003cp>For a firewall request, record the service owner, business reason, exact flow, intended duration, test cases, and rollback. “Allow any/any to see if it works” is a poor substitute for understanding which part of the path failed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","question":"A staff allow rule is below a deny-all rule in a first-match policy. Which wins?","answer":"The earlier matching deny wins. The later allow is never reached for that traffic. Place the approved specific allow correctly, then verify both required access and traffic that must remain blocked.","sources":["firewallGuide","nsgOrder"],"related":["routing","vlans","private-addressing","syslog","security-controls","ftp","smb","rdp"],"keywords":"ACL rule order stateful filtering ports TCP UDP inbound outbound NAT","references":[{"title":"NIST SP 800-41 Rev. 1 — Firewalls and firewall policy","url":"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/41/r1/final"},{"title":"Microsoft — NSG traffic evaluation","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-network/network-security-group-how-it-works"}]},{"slug":"routing","title":"Routing","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"Routing chooses the next hop for an IP packet. Each router uses its own routing information to move the packet toward its destination.","body":"\u003ch2>Where does a packet go next?\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A laptop sending to another subnet needs a path beyond its local network. A \u003cstrong>router\u003c/strong> forwards IP packets between networks. Its \u003cstrong>routing table\u003c/strong> describes destination networks and how to reach them. The \u003cstrong>next hop\u003c/strong> is the next router on the path, or the destination itself when directly reachable on the local link.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A laptop also has routing information. Its \u003cstrong>default gateway\u003c/strong> is commonly the next hop used when no more specific route applies. The router then makes its own forwarding decision. The laptop does not need a complete list of every router between it and a website.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Read the prefix before the metric\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>prefix\u003c/strong>, such as \u003ccode>10.20.30.0/24\u003c/code>, describes a network range. In ordinary destination-based forwarding, choose the \u003cstrong>longest matching prefix\u003c/strong> among the installed routes: the most specific destination range that contains the packet’s destination. A \u003ccode>/24\u003c/code> match beats a \u003ccode>/16\u003c/code> match; either beats the default \u003ccode>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Installed destination\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Fictional path\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">When it is used\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10.20.30.0/24\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Server link\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Addresses from 10.20.30.0 through 10.20.30.255.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10.20.0.0/16\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Branch router\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Other destinations inside 10.20.0.0/16.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Upstream router\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Destinations without a more specific match.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"routing\" aria-label=\"Choose the next hop\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Choose the next hop\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Change the packet destination and remove an installed route. Watch which prefixes match and which one wins.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>10.20.30.10 matches /24, /16, and /0; /24 wins. Remove /24 and the /16 route wins instead. With no matching route, the router cannot forward this packet.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>metric\u003c/strong> helps compare paths according to an operating system or routing protocol’s rules. \u003cstrong>Administrative distance\u003c/strong>, on platforms using that term, ranks route sources when choosing what to install for the same destination prefix. These do not mean a low-cost default route beats a more specific installed route. The exercise omits equal-cost paths and policy-based routing to focus on prefix matching.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>How routes appear\u003c/h2>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Connected routes:\u003c/strong> networks attached to the device’s active interfaces.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Static routes:\u003c/strong> entries an administrator deliberately configures.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Dynamic routes:\u003c/strong> routes learned through protocols such as OSPF or BGP. Those protocols exchange reachability information; they are not needed for every small lab.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2>A route is a path decision, not a guarantee\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The chosen next hop must be reachable, downstream routers need suitable routes, policy must permit the traffic, and a reply needs a return path. A routing entry alone proves none of those. If a specific route disappears and a default route wins, the packet may be sent somewhere that cannot deliver it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For local Ethernet delivery, ARP resolves the next hop’s IPv4 address to a MAC address. For remote traffic, the Ethernet destination changes at each routed link while the final IP destination normally stays the same, except where translation occurs. Read \u003ca href=\"#note-arp\">ARP\u003c/a> to work through that distinction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Troubleshoot an office-to-branch failure\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Record source and destination addresses. Inspect the selected route, outgoing interface, and next hop, then check the return direction. Traceroute-style tools can suggest where replies stop, but some routers filter or limit those replies. A missing hop response is not conclusive evidence that the router cannot forward application traffic.\u003c/p>","question":"A destination matches both 10.20.0.0/16 and 10.20.30.0/24. Which installed route is selected for 10.20.30.10?","answer":"The /24 route, because it is the longest matching prefix. If it is removed, /16 may become the match, but choosing that path does not prove it can deliver the packet.","sources":["routingGuide","arp","connectionTest"],"related":["subnetting","arp","firewalls","vpns","switching","osi"],"keywords":"route table next hop default gateway longest prefix match static dynamic OSPF BGP","references":[{"title":"Cisco — Route selection and longest-prefix matching","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/enhanced-interior-gateway-routing-protocol-eigrp/8651-21.html"},{"title":"RFC 826 — Address Resolution Protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc826"},{"title":"Microsoft — Test-NetConnection","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/nettcpip/test-netconnection"}]},{"slug":"switching","title":"Switching","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"An Ethernet switch learns where devices are connected and forwards frames within a VLAN. It learns from source MAC addresses and looks up destination MAC addresses.","body":"\u003ch2>What a switch actually forwards\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>frame\u003c/strong> is the Ethernet unit delivered across a local link. Its header includes a source and destination \u003cstrong>MAC address\u003c/strong>, the link-layer addresses used for delivery. A \u003cstrong>switch\u003c/strong> connects local devices and decides which port or ports should receive each frame.\u003c/p>\u003cp>At this layer, the switch is making a MAC-based decision within a VLAN. A router makes an IP-based decision between networks. Some managed switches support both Layer 2 switching and Layer 3 routing; identify which function you are troubleshooting rather than guessing from the box’s name.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Learn source, look up destination\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>A frame arrives on a port in a particular VLAN.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The switch learns the source MAC address on that incoming port and VLAN.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It looks up the destination MAC address in its forwarding table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If known on another eligible port, it forwards there. If the destination belongs behind the same incoming port, it does not send the frame back out that port.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the unicast destination is unknown, it normally floods copies to eligible forwarding ports in that VLAN except the incoming port.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Unicast\u003c/strong> means one destination. \u003cstrong>Broadcast\u003c/strong> means all devices in the broadcast domain. An unknown unicast is still addressed to one MAC; copying it across eligible ports does not change that destination into a broadcast address. Devices that receive a frame do not necessarily accept it as their own.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"exercise\" data-exercise=\"switching\" aria-label=\"Teach a switch where devices live\">\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-heading\">\u003cspan class=\"eyebrow\">INTERACTIVE / FICTIONAL EXAMPLE\u003c/span>\u003ch2>Teach a switch where devices live\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Send a frame, then send a reply. Watch the MAC table fill and the highlighted egress ports change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"exercise-mount\">\u003c/div>\u003cnoscript>\u003cp>With an empty table, a staff laptop’s frame to its printer floods eligible VLAN 10 ports. A printer reply teaches the printer’s source MAC and is forwarded to the already learned laptop port. The VLAN 20 guest port receives neither frame.\u003c/p>\u003c/noscript>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2>Ports and VLANs\u003c/h2>\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>access port\u003c/strong> commonly carries one data VLAN to an endpoint. A \u003cstrong>trunk\u003c/strong> carries multiple VLANs between devices, usually using tags to identify them. Voice VLANs, native VLANs, and platform-specific settings add detail; the key is that VLAN membership follows configuration, not which devices happen to share a physical switch.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A switch’s MAC table maps MAC addresses to ports and VLANs. An endpoint’s ARP cache maps IPv4 addresses to MAC addresses. They answer different questions. The switch can learn from an ordinary frame; it does not need to run a fresh ARP query to populate every forwarding entry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Why loops matter\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Redundant links can create a Layer 2 loop. Frames may circulate and multiply, filling the network with traffic. \u003cstrong>Spanning Tree Protocol (STP)\u003c/strong> and its variants help maintain a loop-free forwarding topology by controlling which redundant paths forward. A port that is deliberately not forwarding may be protecting the network. Do not bypass that state without understanding the topology.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A useful switch-port investigation\u003c/h2>\u003cp>Start with link state and the connected device. Check the assigned VLAN, MAC-table entry, interface errors, port-security events, and relevant STP state. If a device gets an address from the wrong subnet, inspect the VLAN and DHCP path. If a link is up but traffic fails, that physical connection alone does not prove the policy, address, and forwarding configuration are correct.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Before changing a remote switch, preserve the configuration and a recovery path. A mistaken uplink or management-VLAN change can disconnect your own support session and many users at once.\u003c/p>","question":"What does a switch learn when a frame arrives, and how is that different from ARP?","answer":"The switch learns the source MAC on the incoming port and VLAN, then looks up the destination MAC. ARP is a separate IPv4-to-MAC resolution process used by endpoints and routers for local delivery.","sources":["switchingGuide","vlan","arp"],"related":["vlans","arp","routing","dhcp","osi"],"keywords":"switch ports MAC table CAM learning flooding unicast broadcast trunk access STP","references":[{"title":"Cisco — Unicast flooding in switched networks","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/switches/catalyst-6000-series-switches/23563-143.html"},{"title":"Cisco — Inter-VLAN routing and access rules","url":"https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/smb/routers/cisco-rv-series-small-business-routers/1393-Inter-VLAN-Routing-with-Targeted-ACL-Restrictions.html"},{"title":"RFC 826 — Address Resolution Protocol","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc826"}]},{"slug":"vpns","title":"VPNs","category":"Networking","kind":"Concept","basis":"Concept study","principle":"A VPN creates a logical connection across another network. In a typical remote-access VPN, an authenticated, encrypted tunnel carries selected traffic between a device and an organization.","body":"\u003ch2>What the tunnel connects\u003c/h2>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Virtual private network\u003c/strong> describes a logical private connection built over another network. A \u003cstrong>remote-access VPN\u003c/strong> connects a user’s device to an organization. A \u003cstrong>site-to-site VPN\u003c/strong> connects network gateways, such as two offices. IPsec and TLS-based VPNs are common approaches, but products differ in authentication, routing, and policy.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Imagine a remote employee who needs a file server on the office network. The home router cannot normally deliver traffic directly to that company’s private address. The VPN provides a tunnel to the company gateway, and routing directs the relevant traffic into it. The server and application still enforce their own access requirements.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Five questions behind “the VPN is connected”\u003c/h2>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Authentication:\u003c/strong> did the expected user or device successfully prove its identity?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Tunnel:\u003c/strong> was the connection established with the expected gateway?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Routing:\u003c/strong> does the destination use the VPN interface?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Name resolution:\u003c/strong> does the internal name resolve to the right address using the intended DNS service?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Permission:\u003c/strong> do network policy and the destination service allow the requested action?\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>A green connection icon mostly answers one part of this chain. It does not establish that every internal server is reachable or that the user can read its files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>Split tunnel and full tunnel\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-scroll\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Design\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Typical behavior\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"col\">Support implication\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Split tunnel\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Configured company destinations use the tunnel; other traffic uses another path.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Inspect the route for the failing destination, not just the tunnel state.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full or force tunnel\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The VPN is the intended default path for traffic.\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Gateway capacity and filtering may affect internet access too; specific routes and exclusions still matter.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Check both IPv4 and IPv6 behavior, DNS handling, and explicit exceptions. “Full tunnel” is a configuration intent, not proof that every possible packet traverses the gateway. See \u003ca href=\"#note-routing\">Routing\u003c/a> for longest-prefix matching.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>A fictional file-share failure\u003c/h2>\u003cp>The VPN connects, but \u003ccode>files.example.internal\u003c/code> fails to resolve. A test to the verified server IP reaches TCP 445. That narrows the immediate issue toward name resolution: check the VPN’s DNS settings and internal name handling. It does not establish that the user has file permissions or that using the IP as a permanent workaround is appropriate.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>What if the name resolves and TCP 445 works, but the share says “Access denied”?\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Move to account and resource authorization: expected identity, group membership, share permissions, and filesystem permissions. Reopening the network path does not grant rights to the files. Record which layer your tests actually passed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\n\u003ch2>Watch for overlapping networks\u003c/h2>\u003cp>If the employee’s home network and the company subnet use the same address range, a more specific local route may send traffic to the home network instead of the company. Check the actual selected route. This is one reason address planning matters beyond a subnetting exercise.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2>VPN, ZTNA, and permissions\u003c/h2>\u003cp>A VPN can provide network access, while a ZTNA service often brokers access to particular applications based on identity and device context. Neither label makes every deployment secure by itself. Configure narrow access, current endpoints, appropriate authentication, monitoring, and ownership. Never assume that tunnel access should also mean administrator access.\u003c/p>","question":"A VPN is connected and a file server answers on TCP 445, but a share returns Access denied. Where should the investigation go next?","answer":"Review the identity used for the share and its share/filesystem permissions. The successful TCP test supports service reachability, not authorization to read files.","sources":["vpnRouting","vpnGuide","smb"],"related":["routing","dns","file-shares","ztna-sase","firewalls","rdp","ssh"],"keywords":"VPN remote access site to site split tunnel force full tunnel IPsec TLS overlapping subnets","references":[{"title":"Microsoft — VPN routing decisions","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/vpn/vpn-routing"},{"title":"Microsoft — VPN technical guide","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/security/operating-system-security/network-security/vpn/vpn-guide"},{"title":"Microsoft — SMB file sharing","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/storage/file-server/file-server-smb-overview"}]}];
